WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.640
- My name is Peter Brooks,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.300
and I just wanna say two minutes worth

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.063
of introduction to this conference.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.860
The questions that we come
together to discuss today

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.910
very often grew out of the
experience of prison teaching

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:28.080
for me and for many of
the others who are here.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.950
In my case, of course, I taught
at East Jersey State Prison

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.190
in Rahway, New Jersey, a few years ago,

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.883
which was for me an absolutely
transformative experience.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.180
To experience as an outsider

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.340
the reality of the inside of prison

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.450
and the way it has affected
so many lives in our country

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.460
is to confront directly the way we

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.430
as a society, as a country,

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:57.500
have created the monster
of mass incarceration.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.210
How has this shaped inner life,

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:03.270
public spectacle, moral possibilities?

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.982
What needs to be thought and
said about incarceration?

00:01:06.982 --> 00:01:10.500
What do the literature
and art of incarceration

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.710
and of the incarcerated have to say to us?

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.480
How does incarceration haunt

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.080
even those outside of prison walls?

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.530
What are the stakes of the
prison education movement?

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.865
These are all questions,
or among the questions

00:01:24.865 --> 00:01:27.270
that we're gonna think about here.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.426
Since I teach literature,

00:01:29.426 --> 00:01:32.630
I began to think about the
place and image of prison

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.120
in the public mind and in
works of literature and art,

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.040
going back at least to the Enlightenment,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.590
especially to the work
of Cesare de Beccaria,

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:45.590
who published his "On Crimes
and Punishments" in 1764,

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.430
if you will, the the very beginning

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.800
of the prison reform movement.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.417
And of other poets, novelist, dramatists,

00:01:52.417 --> 00:01:56.364
artists, composers, who have
meditated on incarceration

00:01:56.364 --> 00:01:58.440
and the human mind.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.764
I think of Stendhal, Silvio
Pellico, Alexander Dumas,

00:02:02.764 --> 00:02:07.470
Dickens, Victor Hugo, Dostoevsky, Gramsci,

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:11.880
and also Beethoven in
his one opera, "Fidelio."

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.010
The French Revolution, you remember,

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.510
kicks off with an attack

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.330
on an infamous prison, the Bastille,

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:22.713
which then symbolically is
demolished stoned by stone.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.180
I think prison has become
the punitive shadow

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.230
to all the major
institutions of our society.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.440
We live in that shadow,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.840
but we also possibly
believe in the promise,

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.566
as one of my students
at East Jersey put it,

00:02:39.566 --> 00:02:43.050
of churning prison into a university,

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.840
a grand aspiration
towards the transformation

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.920
of the carceral space
this country has created

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.950
into a place of learning and creativity

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.380
that brings us together today.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.203
At least that is the hope
that brings us together today.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.330
So we're gonna move at
once to the first panel,

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:04.494
but before I do that,

00:03:04.494 --> 00:03:07.170
I wanna just speak a quick word of thanks

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:10.470
to those who have brought
this symposium into being.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.950
It was originally to have
been at the Eric Holder Center

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.280
at Columbia Law School
two and a half years ago.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.880
It was canceled because of COVID.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.320
It was planned along with my good friends,

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.400
Bernard Harcourt, who is here today,

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:27.450
and Matthew Spellberg,

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.260
who is in Alaska and
couldn't be here today.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.550
Transferred to Yale,

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:37.550
the symposium happily
found new collaborators.

00:03:38.755 --> 00:03:42.467
Richard Weisberg and the Law
and Humanities Foundation

00:03:42.467 --> 00:03:43.416
that he directs,

00:03:43.416 --> 00:03:46.426
Judith Resnick and the Arthur Lyman Center

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:48.420
for Public Interest Law

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.786
that she created at the Yale Law School,

00:03:50.786 --> 00:03:54.930
and Dwayne Betts and his
Freedom Reads program,

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.670
and the Whitney Humanities Center

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.980
where it found his present home

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.530
thanks to Alice Kaplan, the Director,

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.290
and Diane Brown, the Assistant Director,

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.570
and their remarkable staff,

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.843
including Megan O'Donnell
and a number of others.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:15.240
So thanks said, and that's
really a small thanks

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.850
for the great work that
they've done in this.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.081
I'm gonna turn things
over to Emily Bazelon,

00:04:20.081 --> 00:04:23.460
who's too well known to you
all to need introduction.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.150
Among her many honors and
attributes, fortunately for us,

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.150
she's a lecturer and
senior research fellow

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:31.980
at the Yale Law School.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:32.813
Emily.

00:04:34.076 --> 00:04:34.909
(audience applauding)

00:04:34.909 --> 00:04:35.742
- Thank you.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.243
Thanks so much, Peter.

00:04:40.612 --> 00:04:44.220
It is such a pleasure to
be here with all of you

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.100
for this long anticipated exciting event,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.420
which has so much interdisciplinary
richness to offer.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:55.290
I'm really excited for,
to do a lot of listening

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.890
and maybe just a little bit
of talking this morning.

00:04:59.183 --> 00:05:00.947
- Is this on?

00:05:00.947 --> 00:05:02.040
- I don't know, is this working?

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.620
They're all on, okay, good, thank you.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.690
That's okay, thanks, Rachel.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:10.050
So I'm here with an
amazing panel of people

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.282
who bring just a real
variety of perspectives

00:05:12.282 --> 00:05:16.079
to this topic of
incarceration and imagination

00:05:16.079 --> 00:05:20.280
in a multi-genre way.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:24.060
Rachel Kushner, who is sitting
to my right, is a novelist.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.797
Her novels are "Telex from Cuba,"

00:05:26.797 --> 00:05:29.460
"The Flame Throwers," "The Mars Room."

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.800
I have read these books
with great pleasure,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.297
and partly agreed to
participate in this today

00:05:34.297 --> 00:05:36.431
because I was so excited to meet Rachel.

00:05:36.431 --> 00:05:38.370
I really recommend them.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.550
And she has a book of essays
called "The Hard Crowd,"

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.387
and is a contributor to "The New Yorker,"

00:05:43.387 --> 00:05:44.850
"The New York Times Magazine,"

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.557
and a columnist for "Harper's."

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:48.967
Caleb Smith is a professor

00:05:48.967 --> 00:05:51.780
of English and American Studies at Yale.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.296
And his books that are especially relevant

00:05:54.296 --> 00:05:56.040
to today's discussion

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.040
are "The Prison and the
American Imagination,"

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.207
and an edition of Austin Reed's

00:06:01.207 --> 00:06:04.590
"Life of the Adventures
of a Haunted Convict."

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:07.710
And Joy James, who is further to my right,

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:12.300
is the Ebenezer Fitch
Professor of Humanities

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.830
at Williams College,

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:15.548
and the editor of four anthologies

00:06:15.548 --> 00:06:19.323
about political prisoners
and political activists.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:24.270
So I'm delighted to be here
with this fabulous group.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:29.070
And as you can tell, we're
spanning a number of genres here.

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:32.340
This is a group that represents fiction,

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:35.442
thinking about the prison as a space

00:06:35.442 --> 00:06:37.350
in which to create fiction.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:40.230
That is obviously like a
choice that Rachel made

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:41.851
in her third book.

00:06:41.851 --> 00:06:45.660
A choice about setting
that I'm really eager

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.370
to hear more about.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:49.800
And Caleb has written nonfiction,

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.350
and then thought a lot
about first-person accounts

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:55.470
and narratives from people
who are incarcerated,

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.210
which he will talk about more.

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:01.260
And Joy has this perspective
of people who are activists,

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:04.500
both within and outside of prison

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.020
and will be the respondent on the panel.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:11.550
So Rachel, I was hoping that
you would talk a little bit

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.843
about what you think fiction is doing

00:07:14.843 --> 00:07:18.060
in this space of incarceration.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.920
Is it about witnessing?

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.230
And if so, how does it differ?

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.529
What can it offer that's
different from nonfiction

00:07:24.529 --> 00:07:27.690
or kind of first-person
accounts of life in prison,

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.460
which are of course really important,

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:30.877
and we have people in this room today

00:07:30.877 --> 00:07:33.957
who have those perspectives to offer.

00:07:33.957 --> 00:07:35.910
And I'm really interested in how you think

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:37.596
the genre of fiction can compliment

00:07:37.596 --> 00:07:40.650
some of the other writing and work and art

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:41.640
we have in this area.

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:44.643
So can I turn that over
to you with that question?

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:48.840
- Yeah, should we share this?

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:49.673
I don't know.

00:07:49.673 --> 00:07:50.506
- [Caleb] Sure, you can use it first.

00:07:50.506 --> 00:07:51.339
- All right.

00:07:52.833 --> 00:07:53.940
It's like a math problem.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.433
We have four people and three microphones.

00:07:58.153 --> 00:07:59.130
(clearing throat)

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:03.903
Each person gets a microphone
for 1.33% of the time.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.890
Well, I wrote a little paper.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:09.170
Should I just read my paper

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:10.890
or should I answer that question?

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:12.129
- [Emily] Well, you can read your paper

00:08:12.129 --> 00:08:14.869
and hopefully the answers
will emerge at some point.

00:08:14.869 --> 00:08:16.309
We have a while so it's cool.

00:08:16.309 --> 00:08:20.520
- I think that, I think that
what I brought to present

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:21.353
to you all here today

00:08:21.353 --> 00:08:24.499
very much speaks to exactly the points

00:08:24.499 --> 00:08:27.030
that you just brought up.
- [Emily] Good.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.380
- should I just go for it?

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:29.213
- [Emily] Yes.

00:08:30.734 --> 00:08:32.493
- Okay, it's 9:20.

00:08:33.651 --> 00:08:34.740
(chuckling)

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:35.760
I think these are supposed to be

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:37.259
like 10 to 12 minutes, correct?

00:08:37.259 --> 00:08:38.092
- [Emily] Yes.

00:08:38.092 --> 00:08:40.650
All right. (clearing throat)

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:43.140
When I was working on my
novel "The Mars Room,"

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.110
I asked a friend who had
spent 23 years in the prison

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:48.100
on which I modeled my fictional one

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:51.487
if women on death row there worked jobs.

00:08:51.487 --> 00:08:52.957
"They work," she said.

00:08:52.957 --> 00:08:55.627
"They have six sewing machines."

00:08:55.627 --> 00:08:57.517
"What do they sew?" I asked.

00:08:57.517 --> 00:08:59.437
"Sandbags," she said,

00:08:59.437 --> 00:09:02.640
"for the California
Department of Transportation."

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.310
Hearing this, an image
flashed into my thoughts

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:07.983
of an industrial axle turning and turning

00:09:07.983 --> 00:09:10.240
as if I had suddenly been exposed

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:12.870
to the boiler room of the world,

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.360
or rather of California,

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.350
which might as well be the
world with its 40 million.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:20.760
After that conversation,

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:24.150
every time I saw a sandbag
on the side of the highway,

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.198
I felt the turn of this axle

00:09:26.198 --> 00:09:29.400
and I pictured women
running synthetic fabric

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:33.600
along the presser foot of a
state-supplied sewing machine.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.320
These sandbags, which are
everywhere when you start to look,

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.890
remain for me a potent
symbol that the penal system

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:42.903
is floating all over the place.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:45.480
After I wrote "The Mars Room,"

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.970
there was a question
people sometimes asked

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:49.567
that I did not like,

00:09:49.567 --> 00:09:52.978
"Why didn't you write a
nonfiction book about this?"

00:09:52.978 --> 00:09:56.557
I responded mostly by asking, in turn,

00:09:56.557 --> 00:09:59.370
"Is not the novel a parallel universe

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:04.020
where we can glimpse and
understand aspects of this one?"

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:06.840
I think it's no accident
that this symposium

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:10.290
was conceived by someone who
has given so much thought

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:12.000
to the work of Balzac.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:13.560
That's Peter Brooks.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:15.900
Balzac's novels stand as a kind of zenith

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:18.420
of what the 19th century novel can do,

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.910
namely report on social reality

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:22.885
in both its fine grain detail

00:10:22.885 --> 00:10:25.830
and in its sweep and structures.

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.650
Marx and Engels famously turned to Balzac

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:31.860
for what his novels
revealed about capital.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.463
Entire chapters of Thomas Piketty's

00:10:34.463 --> 00:10:37.050
"Capital in the 21st Century"

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:40.170
rely on Balzac novels
for their illumination

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.210
of wealth distribution.

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:45.090
As Peter Brooks notes, Balzac strove

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:47.455
to rival the civil registry

00:10:47.455 --> 00:10:51.540
in his determination to people
his world so thoroughly,

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:53.700
from bankers to peasants to poets

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:58.020
to the figure, of course,
of the criminal male

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:01.440
whose cunning is to find
shortcuts to wealth.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:04.350
Women might be tragic, but only men

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:09.350
like Balzac's infamous anti-hero,
Vautrin, can transgress.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:13.050
The shadow of the criminal in literature

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:15.450
and also movies is long.

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:19.230
It stretches from the 19th
century into the mid 20th

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:22.106
with figures like Andy
Griffith's character

00:11:22.106 --> 00:11:26.820
in "A Face in the Crowd"
discovered in a local jail

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:28.620
who rises to demonic heights

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:31.890
as a Donald Trump-like peddler of serums.

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:34.890
Cool Hand Luke's failure to communicate

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:36.900
with a Florida prison warden,

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:39.150
and Norman Mailer's
version of Gary Gilmore

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:41.220
in the "Executioner's Song."

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:45.840
By 1981, when imprisoned writer
Jack Henry Abbott's letters

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:47.547
to Norman Mailer were published

00:11:47.547 --> 00:11:50.190
as the book "In the Belly of the Beast,"

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.247
this shadow was, I would argue,

00:11:52.247 --> 00:11:55.860
overtaken by a more
engulfing set of shadows-

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:58.590
the ones cast by institutions,

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:03.590
the punitive turn in the
parlance of our symposium prompt.

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:05.430
The reformatory and prisons

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:07.110
that had shaped Jack Henry Abbott

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:10.433
left such a mark on him that
he was free only six weeks

00:12:10.433 --> 00:12:12.990
before he stabbed and killed someone.

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:16.050
Six weeks after that
tragic turn of events,

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:19.200
Michiko Kakutani, then the most powerful

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:20.970
literary critic in America,

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:24.090
condemned Norman Mailer in
a "New York Times" article

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:28.650
titled "The Strange Case of
the Writer and the Criminal."

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:32.640
Kakutani summarized Jack Henry
Abbott's long incarceration

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:34.530
and short, brutal freedom.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:38.070
She blamed Norman Mailer for
the stabbing of the victim,

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:40.260
a young actor named Richard Adan.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:42.870
She talked about Mailer's
obsession with violence

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:46.710
and suggested that writers can
only ever play with violence

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:50.010
and that such play brings consequences.

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.680
Kakutani's argument relies on the idea

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:55.020
that the criminal is a social type,

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:58.380
a member, if you will,
of Balzac's registry.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:02.040
She's unaware and unwilling
to grasp what prison,

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:06.000
even already by 1981,
had become in America-

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:10.050
a way to manage social
problems and inequality.

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:11.940
Prisons draw, as we all know,

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:14.670
from only one layer of society.

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:18.180
Fate of origin and not just destiny

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:22.470
do inform character in life and in novels.

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:25.920
But Kakutani suggests that
Jack Henry Abbott's problem

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:28.830
is that he isn't a reasonable man.

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:30.468
That's the term she uses.

00:13:30.468 --> 00:13:32.880
Her article is fascinating to me

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:34.785
because it is such a transparent attempt

00:13:34.785 --> 00:13:37.620
to exhaustively close a chapter

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.600
of American literary history,

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:41.490
one in which, as she sees it,

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:44.070
writers believe there is a moral symbolism

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:45.792
in crime and punishment.

00:13:45.792 --> 00:13:49.560
But it is Kakutani's
argument itself that is...

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:52.290
Wouldn't it be funny
if she was here today?

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:54.720
But it is Kakutani's argument itself

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:58.350
that is foreclosed and moribund.

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:01.136
By 1981, the United States has embarked

00:14:01.136 --> 00:14:02.970
on a prison building project

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.370
of breathtaking scope and reach.

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:08.580
By then, literary meaning
lies not in the figure

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:10.650
of the so-called criminal,

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:14.250
but in the institutions
that shape this person.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:17.580
To say something true
about a modern day Vautrin,

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:20.570
the novelist must perceive and understand

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:23.703
the institutions that overshadow him.

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:27.540
An interesting echo of Balzac's ambition

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:29.520
to rival the civil registry

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:32.280
can be found in Michel Foucault's hope

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:35.010
to rival a registry of internment

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.080
dating from the 18th century

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:39.753
that he came upon in the
Bibliotechque Nationale.

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:42.390
Foucault planned to write a book

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:45.330
inspired by the brief
descriptions of imprisoned people

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:46.830
in this registry.

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:50.640
His book would be titled
"The Life of Infamous Men."

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:52.200
He described the feeling he got

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.510
upon reading brief and fragmented accounts

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:57.510
of people whose stories
suddenly rose up for him

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:00.360
through two and a half
centuries of silence.

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:04.090
What Foucault aimed to do
was to restore the intensity

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:07.020
of these obscure and
unknown people's lives.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:09.600
Lives, as he put it, reduced to ashes

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:12.900
in the curt tone of a public ledger book.

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:15.060
Foucault describes them as cobbler's,

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:18.037
deserting soldiers, vagabond, monks.

00:15:18.037 --> 00:15:21.217
"All these people might have
stayed in the dark," he says,

00:15:21.217 --> 00:15:23.550
"were it not for the light of power

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:25.200
that shown down on them."

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:27.540
Their moment in court, in prison,

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:30.003
in this registry he holds in his hands.

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:33.270
To write about a prison
is perhaps not unlike

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:36.360
writing about a school, the
culture of a sea vessel,

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:40.320
a hospital or any other
self-enclosed world.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:42.390
In this sense, I see a thread

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:44.070
connecting Melville's "Moby Dick"

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:46.590
to Kesey's "One Flew
Over the Cuckoo's Nest"

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:48.630
and John Cheever's "Falconer."

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:53.580
But much of what sustains a
prison happens outside a prison.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:56.910
The penal system, as I said,
is floating everywhere,

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:59.370
seen or unseen depending.

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:01.740
The writer may have been ensnared in it

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:05.640
and recount from personal
experience, as many have.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:08.820
I never was, but I grew
up with people who were.

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:11.463
Kids whose fathers happened
to be police officers

00:16:11.463 --> 00:16:15.210
while these kids all had a parole officer.

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:17.629
Their mothers worked
at bail bonds companies

00:16:17.629 --> 00:16:20.520
or as court clerks downtown.

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:22.560
Now I live in Los Angeles,

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:24.720
the nation's most populous county.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:28.440
My home is walking distance
from our criminal court complex.

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:31.230
A mile to the east is our jail complex.

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:35.430
What I write as fiction
cannot keep out what I see.

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:38.400
I cannot help but notice
who goes to court, to jail,

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.560
and who works in these places.

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:44.070
This is the world in
which Balzac's Vautrin

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:45.810
now finds himself-

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:49.465
Los Angeles, manufacturing
capital of the US

00:16:49.465 --> 00:16:52.800
and a place of extreme wealth inequality.

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:57.300
A city that spends half of its
operating budget on police.

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:01.140
Vautrin's job prospects
might be a sorting warehouse

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:02.820
in the Inland Empire,

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:05.550
or something higher paying and less legal.

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:08.190
He might be the descendant
of people who came west

00:17:08.190 --> 00:17:10.080
from Texas and Louisiana

00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:13.530
to work unskilled jobs in
the aerospace industry.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:15.930
Jobs that have long vanished.

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:20.930
This new Vautrin is possibly
acquainted with foster care,

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:23.670
youth authority, and family court.

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:25.860
He might try to find a shortcut to wealth

00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:27.810
or a more modestly survival,

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:29.250
but in a modern novel,

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:32.400
he is not counseling Balzac's Rastignac

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:34.410
on marrying rich ladies.

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:37.770
He is boarding a sheriff's department bus

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:41.204
along with 60 other men and
heading for Cocoran Prison

00:17:41.204 --> 00:17:44.160
deep in the heart of industrial farmland

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:48.709
where 6,000 others stand around
on level four housing yards

00:17:48.709 --> 00:17:51.679
overseen by guards for whom prisons

00:17:51.679 --> 00:17:55.260
are one of the biggest
employers in their rural county.

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:58.440
Corcoran is surrounded
by irrigation canals

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:01.200
through which subsidized water flows,

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:02.760
water used to grow almonds

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:04.980
that are exported all over the world.

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:07.260
A crop that is largely automated,

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:11.043
and thus jobs in prisons
rather than farming.

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:15.000
In order to understand
the life of this Vautrin,

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:17.572
the author of the novel
who casts him in a role

00:18:17.572 --> 00:18:21.630
will want to have some
sense of cycles of drought

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:24.990
and economic recession and state capacity.

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:28.135
She'll wanna know how hot
it is on a level four yard

00:18:28.135 --> 00:18:31.590
at Salinas Valley or Chowchilla or Chino.

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:34.290
She'll wanna have glimpsed
the circular logic

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:36.960
of California prison industries

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:39.450
where they make pews for juries

00:18:39.450 --> 00:18:42.960
who decide the fate of people like them.

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:46.590
About her Vautrin, the novelist
will wanna have a sense

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:49.620
of the racial and class
barriers he has faced,

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:53.220
and of how and why her
character might decide

00:18:53.220 --> 00:18:56.070
catastrophically and probably quickly

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:59.918
that violence could be a
solution to his problems.

00:18:59.918 --> 00:19:03.510
This is a modern Vautrin
in modern California.

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:06.417
He has come a long way
from a Balzac novel,

00:19:06.417 --> 00:19:08.130
and he might even be a woman,

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:10.380
which is how I wrote her in my attempt

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:12.080
to depict in my novel "The Mars Room"

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:14.910
the life of someone put on a bus to prison

00:19:14.910 --> 00:19:16.173
in the Central Valley.

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:19.393
I wanna close by coming back

00:19:19.393 --> 00:19:21.970
to this question people have asked me,

00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:26.420
"Why not write a nonfiction
book about this stuff?"

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:30.450
The question isn't really a question.

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:34.770
It's a suggestion that one
should be doing activism

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:36.690
or at the very least,

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:39.340
journalism instead of writing novels

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:41.250
if you're gonna bother people

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:44.253
with such quote/unquote unpleasantness.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:47.125
Foucault proposed project

00:19:47.125 --> 00:19:50.796
of converting the internment
registry into narratives

00:19:50.796 --> 00:19:54.600
anticipated this very reaction.

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:59.480
It is in the infamous of his
title, "Lives of Infamous Men,"

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:03.450
an adjective adding a promise of the lurid

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:07.890
and the sensational, which
I interpret as irony.

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:12.330
The real life outlaw,
even now 300 years ago,

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:16.410
was already buried under the
shadow of the bureaucracies

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:20.638
meant to limit and control
him, to manage him.

00:20:20.638 --> 00:20:25.350
Of the project to restore
this man's intensity,

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:26.700
as Foucault put it,

00:20:26.700 --> 00:20:31.700
Foucault himself said he
lacked the necessary talent.

00:20:31.887 --> 00:20:34.380
He never wrote the book.

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:38.400
I regard this phantom project he proposed

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:42.570
as explicitly for us, the novelists.

00:20:42.570 --> 00:20:45.843
If not to complete, to at least begin.

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:48.309
- [Emily] Thank you.

00:20:48.309 --> 00:20:51.476
(audience applauding)

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:55.620
Caleb, you were gonna talk about,

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:57.420
I think a different kind of writing

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:01.440
from within prisons,
conversion narratives.

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:03.453
So I will turn things over to you.

00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:11.190
- Thank you Emily, Rachel, Joy,

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:13.890
everyone who did the work
to organize this conference.

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:20.090
When I saw the the title for our panel,

00:21:20.377 --> 00:21:23.280
"Literary History of
the Incarcerated Mind,"

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:26.667
that singular really stood
out to me, "the mind."

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:32.250
And since Peter mentioned
some kind of inspiration

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:36.780
for this conference coming
out of his prison teaching,

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:41.427
also in one of my own experiences
with prison teaching--

00:21:42.667 --> 00:21:43.980
- [Audience Member] A
Little closer to mic?

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:45.080
- Sure.

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:45.913
Is that better?

00:21:45.913 --> 00:21:47.370
Can you hear me?

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:50.670
So one of my own experiences
with prison teaching

00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:52.920
has been kind of lingering
in my consciousness

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.630
as I prepared for this event.

00:21:54.630 --> 00:21:57.810
And it was a conversation I
had with a student named Clyde

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:01.110
when I was teaching in
Cheshire Correctional Institute

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:03.030
with Wesleyan's prison program

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:05.463
before we had our own here at Yale.

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:11.250
And we were discussing
Frederick Douglass's

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:13.530
1845 narrative in that class.

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:16.740
And Clyde was, who's kind
of a leader, is Clyde here?

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:20.553
He's here at Yale as a fellow now.

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:23.920
He sort of interrupted the conversation

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:25.590
and he said, "You know,"

00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:28.110
he'd been taking Wesleyan
classes for years at this point.

00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:32.550
And he said, "You know,
people like you come in here,

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:33.756
they want us to read Douglass

00:22:33.756 --> 00:22:35.670
and they want us to read Foucault,

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:39.027
and they wanna ask me
about my subjectivity."

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:41.003
And he said, "What I wanna know is

00:22:41.003 --> 00:22:43.527
what about your subjectivity?"

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:49.940
And that resistance to
being seen as someone

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:53.880
whose subjectivity was
an object of curiosity

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:56.400
and an object of interest on
people who were coming in,

00:22:56.400 --> 00:22:58.473
even presumably to be his teachers,

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:02.550
is something that I have really tried

00:23:02.550 --> 00:23:05.437
to think about the sources of

00:23:05.437 --> 00:23:08.400
and dwell with a little bit.

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:11.580
And I wanted to talk today
about a specific genre

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:15.810
that seems to me very central
to the long literary history

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:19.230
of imagining and attempting to understand

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:22.170
or give accounts of incarcerated minds.

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:24.810
And that is the conversion narrative,

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:28.353
specifically the Protestant
religious conversion narrative.

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:34.997
And some of my research
on the early decades

00:23:34.997 --> 00:23:38.087
and the cultural history
of the US prison system

00:23:38.087 --> 00:23:42.480
in the 18-teens, 20s, 30s, and 40s,

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:46.890
I recently came across
this very remarkable grave

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:49.293
in Canandaigua, New York.

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:54.990
It's a big monument with
a tall obelisk above it

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:57.480
and a weathered metal
plate inscribed on it.

00:23:57.480 --> 00:24:00.003
And the text of the
inscription reads this way,

00:24:01.867 --> 00:24:05.580
"Jacob Hodges, an African Negro

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:07.920
born in poverty and ignorance,

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:12.292
early tempted to sin by
designing and wicked men.

00:24:12.292 --> 00:24:15.360
Once condemned as a felon,

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:18.153
converted by the grace of God in prison,

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:22.866
lived many years a converted
and useful Christian,

00:24:22.866 --> 00:24:27.756
died February, 1842, in
the faith of the gospel

00:24:27.756 --> 00:24:29.877
about 80 years of age."

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:36.510
This monument identifies
the man by his name

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:37.683
and by his race.

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:41.280
And then it tells very
briefly the story of his life.

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:45.120
It goes back to his childhood
in poverty and ignorance,

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:48.360
passes through the crisis of
his temptation and his sin.

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:49.710
Hodges, as it turned out,

00:24:49.710 --> 00:24:52.844
was kind of conscripted by
conspirators to participate

00:24:52.844 --> 00:24:55.740
in the murder of a White property holder

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:57.183
in Orange County, New York.

00:24:58.821 --> 00:25:02.103
It recounts his religious
experience in prison,

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:06.240
and it confirms the lasting effects

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:09.450
of that religious experience.

00:25:09.450 --> 00:25:12.150
When he was converted by
the grace of God in prison,

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:15.270
this grave marker says,
the change in his character

00:25:15.270 --> 00:25:18.720
wasn't just a fleeting
kind of affectation.

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:20.220
It really turned him around for good

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:23.430
and he lived many years this
way in the faith of the gospel.

00:25:23.430 --> 00:25:25.320
So these are the kind of elements

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:27.753
of a Protestant conversion narrative.

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:32.430
First his ignorance, then
his seduction, then his sin,

00:25:32.430 --> 00:25:35.820
then judgment and suffering, conversion,

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:38.280
and this new more faithful
life that he lives out

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:39.530
for the rest of his days.

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:46.040
Above that congregationalist

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:48.778
or Anglo-Protestant conversion narrative,

00:25:48.778 --> 00:25:52.173
there's this Egyptian
symbol, the obelisk, right?

00:25:53.531 --> 00:25:56.430
And I learned a little bit about this

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:59.820
trying to understand it and
and learned that by the,

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:02.708
by about 1842 there were
a number of obelisks

00:26:02.708 --> 00:26:07.260
in the North Atlantic metropoles.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:11.220
And it would be just six years
later that construction began

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:13.830
on the most famous one
in the United States,

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:15.030
the Washington Monument.

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:20.310
So in 1842 in an upstate
New York village cemetery,

00:26:20.310 --> 00:26:23.250
this is not exactly a shocking image,

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:24.480
but it's a sort of,

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:28.620
it is a symbol of a kind of
appropriation of an African form

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:31.860
into this Anglo-American
settler civilization.

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:33.993
The place is called Pioneer Cemetery.

00:26:38.178 --> 00:26:42.480
So it seems plausible to me

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:44.550
that this particular symbol was chosen

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:46.800
partly because as a kind of testament

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:50.160
to the successful racial
assimilation of Jacob Hodges

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:52.530
into this upstate community

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:54.900
by way of his religious conversion,

00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:58.119
or at least to the hope of a
kind of racial assimilation

00:26:58.119 --> 00:27:01.773
in a post-Emancipation
era in New York state.

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:06.783
And then finally there's
the metal plate itself.

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:09.150
I mean obelisks are ancient.

00:27:09.150 --> 00:27:10.950
Protestant conversion narratives go back

00:27:10.950 --> 00:27:14.309
at least to early New England settlements

00:27:14.309 --> 00:27:15.543
on this continent.

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:18.589
But that metal plate
is forged in a foundry

00:27:18.589 --> 00:27:21.840
and it has forged bolts
that are securing it

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:26.673
into the base of this tomb.

00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:30.140
And so in that way,
it's not only an ancient

00:27:30.140 --> 00:27:32.280
or even an old fashioned Christian form,

00:27:32.280 --> 00:27:34.455
but also this tomb is
very much an artifact

00:27:34.455 --> 00:27:36.810
of the new industrial economy

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:39.300
that was taking shape in upstate New York,

00:27:39.300 --> 00:27:44.300
and one of whose major
sites of development

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:49.380
was the contract operated
workshops of Auburn State Prison

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:53.073
where Jacob Hodges was
incarcerated for most of a decade.

00:27:55.170 --> 00:28:00.170
So I take this grave to be
telling one person's story

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:05.590
by way the conversion narrative,

00:28:05.590 --> 00:28:10.590
but also as a kind of
artifact of these three forces

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:13.458
that came together at
the foundational moment

00:28:13.458 --> 00:28:16.113
of modern industrial prison discipline.

00:28:17.190 --> 00:28:21.993
And those are evangelical religion,

00:28:27.060 --> 00:28:29.880
conflicts and struggles over the question

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:33.333
of post-Emancipation racial assimilation.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:37.560
So this is the time of arguments

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:42.510
about colonization
versus education efforts

00:28:42.510 --> 00:28:47.460
to lift up the state's
growing free Black population,

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:48.310
upstate New York.

00:28:49.830 --> 00:28:52.200
And also the industrial economy

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:55.080
that was very quickly
transforming the economy

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:58.050
of upstate and New York
with what historians

00:28:58.050 --> 00:29:01.260
sometimes call the market
revolution and industrialization,

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:04.919
which involved a rapid transition
away from older systems,

00:29:04.919 --> 00:29:05.801
not only of slavery,

00:29:05.801 --> 00:29:10.020
but also of indenture and
apprenticeship toward wage labor

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:12.240
so that the laboring population

00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:14.910
was suddenly much more mobile

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:18.270
and each worker much more on their own.

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:21.390
And many instances of discipline

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:24.180
that previously would've
been handled domestically,

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:25.260
that is by masters,

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:28.110
are now being outsourced to the state,

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:29.579
which is doing them in this,

00:29:29.579 --> 00:29:33.160
doing labor discipline
and moral discipline

00:29:33.160 --> 00:29:35.313
in the new institutions.

00:29:37.503 --> 00:29:41.177
You can see, I think, why
a conversion narrative

00:29:41.177 --> 00:29:43.560
would be useful to a prison system

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:48.063
that explained its project
in terms of rehabilitation.

00:29:50.506 --> 00:29:53.820
It was aligned with the reformist program,

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:57.469
not with vengeance and not
even with mere incapacitation,

00:29:57.469 --> 00:30:00.870
but with the promise
that people's characters

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:03.213
could be rehabilitated in prisons.

00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:08.820
It was also aligned specifically
with religious liberty.

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:12.330
The chaplains who operated
in Auburn State Prison

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:13.890
were not paid by the state.

00:30:13.890 --> 00:30:16.530
They were paid by
philanthropic organizations,

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:20.910
and so your religious education
in a place like Auburn

00:30:20.910 --> 00:30:24.333
could be seen as voluntary,
not state imposed.

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:30.226
And we have much evangelical
activism to this day,

00:30:30.226 --> 00:30:34.800
which understands itself to
be in favor of rehabilitation,

00:30:34.800 --> 00:30:37.077
but not in favor of rehabilitation

00:30:37.077 --> 00:30:40.503
under the direction of
state-appointed elites.

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:44.097
So those are the...

00:30:44.097 --> 00:30:47.550
And you can also see, I think,
the kind of ideological work

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:51.120
that prison conversion
narratives would've done

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:54.990
in the 19th century and
perhaps still do today.

00:30:54.990 --> 00:30:58.470
They give a kind of moral legitimacy

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:02.463
to large scale captivity
and compulsory discipline,

00:31:03.725 --> 00:31:08.725
and specifically tend
to align incarceration,

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:14.940
not with re-enslavement,
not with violence,

00:31:14.940 --> 00:31:17.523
but instead with a kind
of moral correction.

00:31:19.500 --> 00:31:20.863
But over the past 200 years,

00:31:20.863 --> 00:31:23.250
as you can probably imagine,

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:25.530
the conversion narrative
as a kind of template

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:27.150
for telling someone's life story

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:31.200
or telling the story of
someone's experience in prison

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:32.850
going through suffering,

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:35.223
has also been adapted to many other uses.

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:41.880
For criminal justice reformers,

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:44.568
the ideal of rehabilitation,

00:31:44.568 --> 00:31:48.120
and in some cases the ideal
of a kind of conversion

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:50.013
or transformation of one's character,

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:53.622
is often held up as the ideal

00:31:53.622 --> 00:31:57.540
against which other more abusive realities

00:31:57.540 --> 00:31:59.493
and injustices can be measured.

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:02.638
So it can have this kind
of critical function

00:32:02.638 --> 00:32:06.600
in relation to the way
that criminal justice

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:08.133
is actually being enforced.

00:32:09.180 --> 00:32:13.503
It also has been taken up in
more militant and radical ways.

00:32:14.850 --> 00:32:18.458
You can think about autobiographies

00:32:18.458 --> 00:32:23.458
and even shorter pieces of
autobiographical prison writing

00:32:23.970 --> 00:32:26.327
that talk about the author's conversion,

00:32:26.327 --> 00:32:30.836
not to a kind of the docility
of a Christian conversion

00:32:30.836 --> 00:32:33.870
and acceptance of the justice
of their own sentence,

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:36.250
but instead to a kind
of militant opposition

00:32:36.250 --> 00:32:40.293
to the systems hat have
seized them, right?

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:43.602
We can think about conversions to Marxism,

00:32:43.602 --> 00:32:45.840
conversions to radical Islam,

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:49.893
conversions to faiths and positions

00:32:49.893 --> 00:32:52.770
that happen through the prison experience,

00:32:52.770 --> 00:32:56.790
but that set the author
now against the system

00:32:56.790 --> 00:32:58.413
that has condemned them.

00:33:02.121 --> 00:33:06.844
For people like me who
work in literary studies

00:33:06.844 --> 00:33:08.820
and cultural studies,

00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:12.480
these documents have a real richness.

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:14.460
They are available to

00:33:14.460 --> 00:33:17.103
humanist kinds of reading and criticism.

00:33:18.810 --> 00:33:21.480
We may have a sense in reading them

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:23.340
that we've been able to recover

00:33:23.340 --> 00:33:25.320
or at least acknowledge voices

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:28.443
against the silencing power of the state.

00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:33.717
We might encounter real narratives

00:33:36.345 --> 00:33:40.200
of psychological and spiritual vividness

00:33:40.200 --> 00:33:42.090
in these personal accounts.

00:33:42.090 --> 00:33:44.280
The traditional, the kind of irony

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:46.080
of the traditional conversion narrative

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:47.550
is that it was supposed to be your own

00:33:47.550 --> 00:33:49.319
intensely sincere experience,

00:33:49.319 --> 00:33:51.360
but it wasn't a real conversion narrative

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:53.237
unless it conformed
perfectly to the template

00:33:53.237 --> 00:33:56.630
of the conversion narrative, right?

00:33:56.630 --> 00:34:00.294
But people have, but of
course developed stories

00:34:00.294 --> 00:34:03.630
about conversion that
have a lot of idiosyncrasy

00:34:03.630 --> 00:34:06.783
and richness and
intensity and singularity.

00:34:09.270 --> 00:34:13.320
We can read these texts
for moments in which they

00:34:13.320 --> 00:34:18.320
seem to resist or subvert
or even just improvise

00:34:18.660 --> 00:34:21.270
against what seems to be the script

00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:23.640
that's been provided for them to perform,

00:34:23.640 --> 00:34:27.390
and so locate moments of agency,

00:34:27.390 --> 00:34:29.973
locate moments of subversion and so on.

00:34:31.890 --> 00:34:35.010
It is a genre that has a long history then

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:38.163
and many uses and much richness.

00:34:40.260 --> 00:34:43.520
At the same time, it has its limits

00:34:43.520 --> 00:34:48.153
or certain ways of approaching
it can be limiting.

00:34:49.140 --> 00:34:54.140
And specifically, one reason
I'm glad to be thinking here

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:57.030
with Rachel's account
of what novels can do

00:34:57.030 --> 00:34:59.265
is that much of what she's just described

00:34:59.265 --> 00:35:02.730
in terms of the sweep of a social world

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:05.403
is what falls away in
a conversion narrative.

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:08.973
The incarcerated mind, right?

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:12.633
The conversion narrative itself,

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:18.300
may be part of its ideological
function is to say,

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:19.950
hey, the prison isn't so bad.

00:35:19.950 --> 00:35:22.560
It actually gave me this
opportunity to correct myself

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:24.780
and rehabilitate myself and so on.

00:35:24.780 --> 00:35:28.140
But another part of its
ideological function is to say

00:35:28.140 --> 00:35:29.403
here is one story.

00:35:31.050 --> 00:35:32.910
One person's story, and
that's what prison is.

00:35:32.910 --> 00:35:34.980
It's one person's story, right?

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:39.980
And so the large sweep and
scale of the catastrophe

00:35:41.190 --> 00:35:42.270
may drop out of view

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:45.783
if we think about the
singularity of this voice.

00:35:48.030 --> 00:35:53.030
How then do we scale up from
this one story to the mass,

00:35:54.960 --> 00:35:57.870
to the system, to the structure?

00:35:57.870 --> 00:35:59.070
I think this is a problem

00:35:59.070 --> 00:36:00.794
in how we think about
conversion narratives,

00:36:00.794 --> 00:36:03.420
and in some ways I think
it's a problem at the heart

00:36:03.420 --> 00:36:05.736
of how much the humanities themselves

00:36:05.736 --> 00:36:09.003
can say or do in relation
to these systems.

00:36:11.310 --> 00:36:14.100
But what I've tried to
at least sketch out here,

00:36:14.100 --> 00:36:16.020
at least at the beginning,

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:21.020
is ways of reading these not
only in order to acknowledge

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:26.310
and value the particular
stories that they tell,

00:36:26.310 --> 00:36:27.643
but also to move out from them

00:36:27.643 --> 00:36:31.227
into the scales and structures
of historical forces

00:36:31.227 --> 00:36:34.290
that give them weight
and give them meaning.

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:37.410
And the three in particular
that seem resonant to me,

00:36:37.410 --> 00:36:42.410
that seem almost to have
come out of frozenness

00:36:43.470 --> 00:36:46.230
and reanimated themselves in our own time

00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:47.460
are the three that I mentioned

00:36:47.460 --> 00:36:51.270
shaping the tomb of Jacob
Hodges at the beginning.

00:36:51.270 --> 00:36:54.640
We have a very active evangelical,

00:36:54.640 --> 00:36:58.890
White evangelical movement
aligned with the politics

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:01.530
of harsh punishment in
our country right now,

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:03.741
just as they did in the
burned-over district

00:37:03.741 --> 00:37:07.473
of upstate New York during the
Great Awakening of the 1840s.

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:11.870
We have a radical shift
and destabilization

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:14.010
of our economic system.

00:37:14.010 --> 00:37:16.590
For them it was the
transition from indenture,

00:37:16.590 --> 00:37:18.120
apprenticeship, and slavery

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:20.703
toward industrialism and wage labor.

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:25.193
For us, it is the other
side of de-industrialization

00:37:25.193 --> 00:37:28.830
that's been analyzed by
Ruthie Wilson Gilmore

00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:31.920
and many others as being
the economic context

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:34.623
within which this system takes shape.

00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:39.680
And we have a kind of moral panic

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:43.620
about racialized migrant
laboring populations

00:37:43.620 --> 00:37:46.200
just as they did in upstate
New York in the 1840s,

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:48.600
and conflicts over whether there will be

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:51.720
a kind of assimilation overseen

00:37:51.720 --> 00:37:54.510
by White Christian nationalist authorities

00:37:54.510 --> 00:37:56.460
or whether there can only be exclusion,

00:37:56.460 --> 00:37:58.710
separation, and expulsion.

00:37:58.710 --> 00:38:03.180
And reading for those political
forces is one way, I think,

00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:07.680
we can both see the singularity
of the conversion narrative

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:09.750
and of the voice in it,

00:38:09.750 --> 00:38:12.330
and also think about some
of the larger political

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:14.490
and cultural forces that give it its force

00:38:14.490 --> 00:38:15.450
and give it its meaning

00:38:15.450 --> 00:38:18.690
and toward which we have
to gain some leverage.

00:38:18.690 --> 00:38:19.523
Thanks.

00:38:21.060 --> 00:38:21.893
- [Emily] Thank you.

00:38:21.893 --> 00:38:24.902
(audience applauding)

00:38:28.080 --> 00:38:29.040
- [Emily] Can I hand it over to you?

00:38:29.040 --> 00:38:29.873
- Yeah, sure.

00:38:29.873 --> 00:38:30.706
- [Emily] Great.

00:38:30.706 --> 00:38:32.130
- Okay, good morning.

00:38:32.130 --> 00:38:33.930
Thank you. (clearing throat)

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:35.970
Excuse me, thank you, Rachel and Caleb.

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:37.260
It's really rich.

00:38:37.260 --> 00:38:38.850
So I've got five minutes,

00:38:38.850 --> 00:38:40.113
I'm gonna have to squash.

00:38:41.640 --> 00:38:45.366
There's a lot in both of
your, you know, contributions,

00:38:45.366 --> 00:38:49.050
and I started thinking, excuse me.

00:38:49.050 --> 00:38:51.213
I started thinking of dichotomies,

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:59.040
and the possibility of what
could be synthesized, right?

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:02.280
So you have fiction, you have nonfiction,

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:06.030
you have this subject
gazing at the object,

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:08.012
meaning the incarcerated.

00:39:08.012 --> 00:39:08.845
(coughing)

00:39:08.845 --> 00:39:11.370
I also wanted to add the possibility

00:39:11.370 --> 00:39:13.170
that there's the focus on the individual,

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:16.260
but there's also the collective
and the communal, right?

00:39:16.260 --> 00:39:19.740
That whole communities can be decimated

00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:21.420
and then reconstituted.

00:39:21.420 --> 00:39:24.420
That fathers will meet
their sons incarcerated,

00:39:24.420 --> 00:39:26.520
you know, mothers who have long terms

00:39:26.520 --> 00:39:28.200
can meet their children.

00:39:28.200 --> 00:39:30.450
Or we spoke about it last night,

00:39:30.450 --> 00:39:32.790
give birth inside of these systems

00:39:32.790 --> 00:39:36.360
and then have their child
removed and disappeared, right?

00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:41.190
So I'm wondering how you
see the convergence, right,

00:39:41.190 --> 00:39:44.730
as a conversion narrative, right?

00:39:44.730 --> 00:39:46.650
So I mean, Caleb,

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:49.380
you laid out what we've inherited

00:39:49.380 --> 00:39:51.167
and what we will leave our children.

00:39:51.167 --> 00:39:52.342
Hopefully it will improve,

00:39:52.342 --> 00:39:56.250
but you know, the odds don't
seems so great at the moment.

00:39:56.250 --> 00:39:58.743
So Kathy Belew, who's at
University of Chicago,

00:39:58.743 --> 00:40:02.580
has a text on how the White
supremacist underground

00:40:02.580 --> 00:40:03.602
came above ground,

00:40:03.602 --> 00:40:07.680
and these long wars that the
US had in the Middle East

00:40:07.680 --> 00:40:11.010
actually professionalize
them as an army, right?

00:40:11.010 --> 00:40:14.340
And so it's not just what
happens in the voting space.

00:40:14.340 --> 00:40:16.350
It's also what happens with people

00:40:16.350 --> 00:40:17.572
who I think you were pointing to

00:40:17.572 --> 00:40:20.245
when you're talking about
this increasing animus

00:40:20.245 --> 00:40:24.210
against migration, right?

00:40:24.210 --> 00:40:26.700
From sites that have been devastated

00:40:26.700 --> 00:40:29.063
by the over-consumption of water,

00:40:29.063 --> 00:40:31.761
and, you know, environment devastation

00:40:31.761 --> 00:40:33.930
done largely by the West.

00:40:33.930 --> 00:40:36.660
So if there is a convergence,

00:40:36.660 --> 00:40:40.260
what might that look like
as a political phenomena?

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:42.570
And also what are the possibilities

00:40:42.570 --> 00:40:44.816
that as much as we emphasize morality,

00:40:44.816 --> 00:40:49.816
we need a shared understanding
of political ethics, right?

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:52.560
So how we wanna be as moral people

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:54.690
is that we care about the incarcerated,

00:40:54.690 --> 00:40:55.570
which is, you know,

00:40:55.570 --> 00:40:57.056
and I really heard you when you said,

00:40:57.056 --> 00:40:59.400
well, you know, people are saying,

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:02.559
well, why don't you do
nonfiction and be useful, right?

00:41:02.559 --> 00:41:05.340
Which of course is a false dichotomy

00:41:05.340 --> 00:41:08.460
because your work is abolitionist work.

00:41:08.460 --> 00:41:10.530
And people who are incarcerated,

00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:12.840
at least the few times I've
been able to be inside,

00:41:12.840 --> 00:41:16.590
they are also writers and
authors and they read novels

00:41:16.590 --> 00:41:18.166
and they write novels, they write poetry,

00:41:18.166 --> 00:41:20.190
they write memoirs.

00:41:20.190 --> 00:41:21.690
But I'm wondering, you know,

00:41:21.690 --> 00:41:25.050
as academics and people in
this room are also writers,

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:28.509
to what extent do we have
a community of writers,

00:41:28.509 --> 00:41:31.740
whatever genre we explore or, you know,

00:41:31.740 --> 00:41:35.127
dabble in or become professionalized
in, are brilliant in,

00:41:35.127 --> 00:41:38.487
that it actually constitutes
a political ethics

00:41:38.487 --> 00:41:41.610
that can bring another kind
of conversion narrative

00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:43.708
other than the evangelical reactionaries

00:41:43.708 --> 00:41:45.900
that are on the rise?

00:41:45.900 --> 00:41:49.950
And I'm not sure like that we've agreed

00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:52.560
to even attempt that endeavor.

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:53.850
Like these are all peers.

00:41:53.850 --> 00:41:55.827
The incarcerated writers
are our peers, right,

00:41:55.827 --> 00:41:57.153
and our teachers.

00:41:58.950 --> 00:42:01.233
And I wonder what would it take,

00:42:03.210 --> 00:42:04.560
you know, to push forward with that?

00:42:04.560 --> 00:42:06.650
And I just wanna give
a couple like specifics

00:42:06.650 --> 00:42:08.950
'cause I know I like, I
have two minutes left.

00:42:10.200 --> 00:42:12.320
So I was thinking of, you know,

00:42:12.320 --> 00:42:14.160
you brought up Foucault.

00:42:14.160 --> 00:42:16.770
And Foucault visited Attica.

00:42:16.770 --> 00:42:20.063
And Foucault, also under the
encouragement of Jean Genet,

00:42:20.063 --> 00:42:24.030
wrote a pamphlet that Gallimard published.

00:42:24.030 --> 00:42:26.790
It was in French til
like a decade or so ago.

00:42:26.790 --> 00:42:28.350
I think it was called "The Assassination"

00:42:28.350 --> 00:42:30.510
or "The Masked Assassination,"

00:42:30.510 --> 00:42:33.210
and he was talking about
George Jackson's death.

00:42:33.210 --> 00:42:35.820
So this would be someone
who needed a conversion,

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:36.720
but it wasn't gonna be

00:42:36.720 --> 00:42:38.835
into a certain kind of religiosity.

00:42:38.835 --> 00:42:42.870
It was gonna be a conversion
to a certain kind of militancy

00:42:42.870 --> 00:42:44.760
aligned with the Black Panther Party.

00:42:44.760 --> 00:42:47.400
And I would argue that
the state and society

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:49.740
doesn't want those kind of conversions.

00:42:49.740 --> 00:42:53.300
So if we...

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:55.740
I don't, you know, I can't, I mean,

00:42:55.740 --> 00:42:57.573
I've got 60 seconds now.

00:42:59.040 --> 00:43:02.070
But I'm really troubled
by the ethics of this.

00:43:02.070 --> 00:43:07.020
Like what we don't want is
what will continue to manifest

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:10.800
because of the violence arrayed
against certain communities.

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:12.630
So do we have a tolerance

00:43:12.630 --> 00:43:16.320
of interacting with what
we don't want, right?

00:43:16.320 --> 00:43:19.530
To stitch together sort of
common political ethics.

00:43:19.530 --> 00:43:21.270
So Jean Genet wrote the forward

00:43:21.270 --> 00:43:23.910
to George Jackson's book
at the encouragement

00:43:23.910 --> 00:43:26.010
of Fay Stender, who is an attorney.

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:27.245
You know, kind of recruited him.

00:43:27.245 --> 00:43:28.740
You know, this White intellectual,

00:43:28.740 --> 00:43:30.570
sort of like I want you to, you know,

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:33.570
my legal degree is from
the University Chicago.

00:43:33.570 --> 00:43:34.740
You're Jean Genet.

00:43:34.740 --> 00:43:37.770
I want you to do this for this
book that's very rebellious.

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:39.690
And he agreed to do that, right?

00:43:39.690 --> 00:43:43.440
James Baldwin will write an
open letter for Angela Davis

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:44.520
when she's on trial,

00:43:44.520 --> 00:43:46.840
and before increasingly the populations

00:43:46.840 --> 00:43:48.990
can say we can embrace her.

00:43:48.990 --> 00:43:49.860
We understand that she

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:53.340
has a certain kind of
innocence and legitimacy.

00:43:53.340 --> 00:43:55.830
But there are people who are rebels

00:43:55.830 --> 00:43:57.930
and there are people who are not innocent,

00:43:57.930 --> 00:44:00.240
and they continue as our peers.

00:44:00.240 --> 00:44:04.890
So if we were to recognize
that, what would we become?

00:44:04.890 --> 00:44:08.013
And then how does that,
you know, subject-object,

00:44:09.270 --> 00:44:11.437
you know, dichotomy become a synthesis

00:44:11.437 --> 00:44:14.290
and the fiction-nonfiction can emerge

00:44:14.290 --> 00:44:18.690
and too just like literary contributions

00:44:18.690 --> 00:44:20.970
that would allow us to
have more strategies

00:44:20.970 --> 00:44:23.343
and more tools for what we need to do?

00:44:25.976 --> 00:44:27.813
- I love that, thank you.

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:32.250
I'm gonna add a little
bit to this discussion

00:44:32.250 --> 00:44:35.692
'cause it's hitting so many
notes from my own work,

00:44:35.692 --> 00:44:37.653
so I can't quite resist.

00:44:38.641 --> 00:44:41.550
Caleb, you were talking
about the American mind,

00:44:41.550 --> 00:44:42.540
and I think in...

00:44:42.540 --> 00:44:44.250
Or I'm sorry, the incarcerated mind.

00:44:44.250 --> 00:44:45.630
It could also be the American mind.

00:44:45.630 --> 00:44:48.120
And I always pluralize words like that

00:44:48.120 --> 00:44:49.740
because to me as a journalist,

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:53.880
one of the most difficult
aspects of my work

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:56.100
is figuring out how to use a story,

00:44:56.100 --> 00:44:57.810
a narrative to say something

00:44:57.810 --> 00:45:01.500
about this scaled up catastrophe.

00:45:01.500 --> 00:45:03.120
And you know, Joy,

00:45:03.120 --> 00:45:06.033
you're taking this in another direction.

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:08.160
Not just multiplicity,

00:45:08.160 --> 00:45:10.290
but thinking of people as peers

00:45:10.290 --> 00:45:14.223
as opposed to necessarily the
subject-object relationship.

00:45:15.120 --> 00:45:18.237
And when you are a writer
who's not yourself incarcerated

00:45:18.237 --> 00:45:20.100
and you're writing about other people,

00:45:20.100 --> 00:45:23.100
there is this kind of
way in which they are,

00:45:23.100 --> 00:45:27.330
if not the object in a,
you know, abstract way,

00:45:27.330 --> 00:45:29.003
you are trying to understand their world.

00:45:29.003 --> 00:45:31.260
You are not within it.

00:45:31.260 --> 00:45:33.750
And I think the reason, to me,

00:45:33.750 --> 00:45:37.325
that's so obvious for art or
novels or fiction or movies

00:45:37.325 --> 00:45:40.980
about incarcerated people and environments

00:45:40.980 --> 00:45:42.480
and the communities they're parts of

00:45:42.480 --> 00:45:45.417
is that hopefully those
are things that move people

00:45:45.417 --> 00:45:48.480
who are outside this self-enclosed world

00:45:48.480 --> 00:45:49.617
that you were talking about, Rachel.

00:45:49.617 --> 00:45:51.330
And so there's a parallel universe

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:55.920
that pulls in more
people's hearts and minds.

00:45:55.920 --> 00:45:58.380
But the step to thinking
about people as peers

00:45:58.380 --> 00:46:01.200
is a more radical and profound step

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:05.220
that I think, you know,
as a society we have,

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:08.370
we continue to fail at
almost all the time.

00:46:08.370 --> 00:46:09.930
I've been thinking about it personally

00:46:09.930 --> 00:46:12.780
because I wrote a story for
"The New York Times Magazine"

00:46:12.780 --> 00:46:13.613
a couple years ago,

00:46:13.613 --> 00:46:15.960
or actually a year and a half ago,

00:46:15.960 --> 00:46:18.674
about a man named Yutico Briley,

00:46:18.674 --> 00:46:20.836
who wrote to me from prison.

00:46:20.836 --> 00:46:25.530
And he wrote a short,
but very clear letter.

00:46:25.530 --> 00:46:27.580
I get a lot of mail from
incarcerated people.

00:46:27.580 --> 00:46:32.550
Lots of lawyers and journalists
and even professors do.

00:46:32.550 --> 00:46:34.170
And it's hard to know what to make

00:46:34.170 --> 00:46:35.850
of this multiplicity of voices.

00:46:35.850 --> 00:46:37.860
It can feel sort of overwhelming.

00:46:37.860 --> 00:46:40.394
But this letter touched me
for a couple of reasons.

00:46:40.394 --> 00:46:44.010
There was a claim of innocence
that Yutico made in it,

00:46:44.010 --> 00:46:46.609
but he also said that he
was imprisoned for 60 years

00:46:46.609 --> 00:46:48.480
for an armed robbery,

00:46:48.480 --> 00:46:51.270
and nobody had been injured
in that armed robbery.

00:46:51.270 --> 00:46:53.820
And so my initial response was to think,

00:46:53.820 --> 00:46:55.950
well, that was so drastically unfair.

00:46:55.950 --> 00:46:58.440
Such an example of excessive punishment,

00:46:58.440 --> 00:46:59.700
which is something Dwayne Betts,

00:46:59.700 --> 00:47:01.410
who unfortunately isn't
here with us today,

00:47:01.410 --> 00:47:03.028
but something he talks and thinks

00:47:03.028 --> 00:47:05.250
and has taught me a lot about.

00:47:05.250 --> 00:47:07.140
So I reached out to Yutico.

00:47:07.140 --> 00:47:10.860
We started corresponding through JPay,

00:47:10.860 --> 00:47:14.107
the sort of corrupt for-profit service

00:47:14.107 --> 00:47:16.590
that is what a lot of incarcerated people

00:47:16.590 --> 00:47:18.147
are forced to rely on.

00:47:18.147 --> 00:47:21.270
And Yutico was sort of
having to reel me in, right,

00:47:21.270 --> 00:47:22.740
and interest me.

00:47:22.740 --> 00:47:25.890
There was this way in which he stood to,

00:47:25.890 --> 00:47:29.760
he had to figure out a way to
persuade me to care about him.

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:32.430
And then over the course of time

00:47:32.430 --> 00:47:34.830
I got more involved in this case.

00:47:34.830 --> 00:47:36.347
I ended up looking for a lawyer for him

00:47:36.347 --> 00:47:38.760
'cause it was so clear
to me that what he needed

00:47:38.760 --> 00:47:42.330
was a lawyer, not a
journalist at this moment.

00:47:42.330 --> 00:47:44.400
That lawyer happened to be my sister.

00:47:44.400 --> 00:47:47.370
So then this trying to
figure out, you know,

00:47:47.370 --> 00:47:50.940
whether there was a
way to exonerate Yutico

00:47:50.940 --> 00:47:53.190
years after he'd been incarcerated

00:47:53.190 --> 00:47:55.050
became this kind of joint project.

00:47:55.050 --> 00:47:57.900
And Yutico is a very charismatic person,

00:47:57.900 --> 00:48:01.200
so his sort of personhood
was really important

00:48:01.200 --> 00:48:03.690
to what we were working on.

00:48:03.690 --> 00:48:07.080
And he was really in a
lot of ways he was driving

00:48:07.080 --> 00:48:08.250
what was happening,

00:48:08.250 --> 00:48:12.003
but also of course had kind
of limited agency necessarily.

00:48:13.320 --> 00:48:17.700
So in the end he was
not guilty of this crime

00:48:17.700 --> 00:48:21.210
he'd been convicted of,
and he was exonerated

00:48:21.210 --> 00:48:23.970
in part because the community
in New Orleans changed.

00:48:23.970 --> 00:48:27.480
The voters decided to elect
a new district attorney

00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:28.950
and a new judge,

00:48:28.950 --> 00:48:32.670
who were much more
interested in actual justice

00:48:32.670 --> 00:48:35.130
than the people who'd preceded them.

00:48:35.130 --> 00:48:40.130
And so Yutico got out of
prison, was exonerated.

00:48:40.230 --> 00:48:42.750
It's now about a year and a half later,

00:48:42.750 --> 00:48:45.000
and I started getting more mail

00:48:45.000 --> 00:48:47.610
after my piece came out, not surprisingly,

00:48:47.610 --> 00:48:50.975
and I couldn't bear to just
not respond to the letters

00:48:50.975 --> 00:48:53.700
in the way that I had
tended to do in the past.

00:48:53.700 --> 00:48:55.530
So I started a project here at Yale

00:48:55.530 --> 00:48:57.840
in which law students
and I have been working

00:48:57.840 --> 00:48:59.794
with these letters to create a database

00:48:59.794 --> 00:49:03.210
that's on the Freedom Reads website

00:49:03.210 --> 00:49:06.180
and is trying to amplify the
voices of incarcerated people.

00:49:06.180 --> 00:49:09.360
And it's very much about a
kind of multiplicity of voices,

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:12.540
and hoping that, this is
something Dwayne says a lot,

00:49:12.540 --> 00:49:15.330
that if people could
see and hear the voices

00:49:15.330 --> 00:49:16.199
that we see and hear,

00:49:16.199 --> 00:49:20.010
if you're in touch with
people on the inside,

00:49:20.010 --> 00:49:24.990
that maybe that would create
the kind of energy for change

00:49:24.990 --> 00:49:28.410
in radical form that we are still lacking.

00:49:28.410 --> 00:49:31.053
It's not quite a peer relationship though.

00:49:31.950 --> 00:49:34.947
And yet I have ended up having
exactly that relationship

00:49:34.947 --> 00:49:37.156
with someone who's incarcerated

00:49:37.156 --> 00:49:40.050
whose name is John J. Lennon,

00:49:40.050 --> 00:49:43.230
who's a prison journalist
who Dwayne introduced me to.

00:49:43.230 --> 00:49:46.170
And John and I have been
writing together a newsletter

00:49:46.170 --> 00:49:48.840
for "The New York Times"
about some of these stories

00:49:48.840 --> 00:49:49.680
that are coming in,

00:49:49.680 --> 00:49:52.440
and it's really turned
into a collaboration.

00:49:52.440 --> 00:49:54.660
I don't think I really
thought it through very much

00:49:54.660 --> 00:49:55.890
when Dwayne proposed it.

00:49:55.890 --> 00:49:57.470
I was sort of glad to feel like

00:49:57.470 --> 00:50:00.330
maybe I was supporting John in some way,

00:50:00.330 --> 00:50:02.911
but that is not the way the
relationship has developed.

00:50:02.911 --> 00:50:07.530
John turns out to have
lots of ideas to offer,

00:50:07.530 --> 00:50:09.450
but also incredible patience.

00:50:09.450 --> 00:50:11.278
So we had this period over the summer

00:50:11.278 --> 00:50:14.481
where my editors were not
really sure what shape

00:50:14.481 --> 00:50:17.520
they wanted the first edition
of this newsletter to take,

00:50:17.520 --> 00:50:20.817
and I was getting really impatient
with them and frustrated.

00:50:20.817 --> 00:50:24.870
And John was the one who really kind of

00:50:24.870 --> 00:50:27.002
saw through my frustration

00:50:27.002 --> 00:50:29.820
and was able to say, you know what?

00:50:29.820 --> 00:50:32.247
Like they are trying to make
this as good as it can be

00:50:32.247 --> 00:50:33.976
and we need to take their feedback

00:50:33.976 --> 00:50:38.406
and really incorporate it
and think about, you know,

00:50:38.406 --> 00:50:41.010
why they're having us change course.

00:50:41.010 --> 00:50:44.310
And he was by far the more mature one

00:50:44.310 --> 00:50:46.680
in this interaction of the two of us,

00:50:46.680 --> 00:50:48.540
which shouldn't have really surprised me,

00:50:48.540 --> 00:50:52.020
and yet it really turned the
tables on my own assumptions

00:50:52.020 --> 00:50:54.661
about who peers can be

00:50:54.661 --> 00:50:57.300
and what those kinds of
relationships can be.

00:50:57.300 --> 00:50:59.610
So I guess I'm telling this story

00:50:59.610 --> 00:51:03.590
because it seems to me that
what we're hearing up here

00:51:03.590 --> 00:51:07.560
is this sort of grappling with
what it is to tell stories,

00:51:07.560 --> 00:51:10.380
to figure out a way how to
scale them up to a recognition

00:51:10.380 --> 00:51:13.140
of the catastrophe you
were talking about, Caleb.

00:51:13.140 --> 00:51:15.360
But then also figuring out this move

00:51:15.360 --> 00:51:18.000
where we're not just sort
of bestowing attention.

00:51:18.000 --> 00:51:21.884
We're also really seeing
people as individual humans

00:51:21.884 --> 00:51:24.090
and as just like us,

00:51:24.090 --> 00:51:27.330
just in a different self-enclosed world.

00:51:27.330 --> 00:51:30.720
So that was a little bit
of a lot of thinking.

00:51:30.720 --> 00:51:34.500
But I wonder, Rachel, if that
speaks to some of your work?

00:51:34.500 --> 00:51:36.029
- Well, yeah.

00:51:36.029 --> 00:51:41.029
I'm still thinking about
what Joy was saying.

00:51:41.340 --> 00:51:45.810
Like so many incredibly
incisive points she made

00:51:45.810 --> 00:51:48.240
that I immediately wanted to respond to

00:51:48.240 --> 00:51:50.700
and then incorporate what
you just said, Emily.

00:51:50.700 --> 00:51:53.490
But so back to Jean Genet
just for a quick second.

00:51:53.490 --> 00:51:54.323
I'm so glad you mentioned him

00:51:54.323 --> 00:51:59.323
because actually in that
incredibly damning article,

00:51:59.528 --> 00:52:00.883
self-damning maybe,

00:52:00.883 --> 00:52:04.941
piece that Kakutani wrote in 1981,

00:52:04.941 --> 00:52:06.783
she quotes Joyce Carol Oates,

00:52:06.783 --> 00:52:09.716
who says that actually Jack Henry Abbott

00:52:09.716 --> 00:52:14.190
was not a real criminal
or an authentic criminal,

00:52:14.190 --> 00:52:15.930
and she uses the word authentic,

00:52:15.930 --> 00:52:17.100
because he seemed like somebody

00:52:17.100 --> 00:52:19.110
who'd read a lot of Jean Genet.

00:52:19.110 --> 00:52:23.100
And so she's desperate
to keep writers over here

00:52:23.100 --> 00:52:24.810
and criminals over here,

00:52:24.810 --> 00:52:27.210
and anybody who smells like literature

00:52:27.210 --> 00:52:29.334
cannot come from the streets.

00:52:29.334 --> 00:52:33.030
And it's just, it's
wonderfully transparent

00:52:33.030 --> 00:52:35.520
or horribly transparent.

00:52:35.520 --> 00:52:39.840
In my own experiences working
with people inside prison,

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:43.260
which I do separate from writing novels,

00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:46.260
I have peers who are
serving life sentences.

00:52:46.260 --> 00:52:49.290
In one case, somebody who's
serving two life sentences.

00:52:49.290 --> 00:52:54.290
Meaning when she goes up for
parole after her 25 years,

00:52:54.976 --> 00:52:58.230
if the parole board deems
her suitable for freedom,

00:52:58.230 --> 00:53:00.720
then she can start her second sentence.

00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:03.924
And a lot of these people are readers.

00:53:03.924 --> 00:53:05.885
I've one friend in prison who told me that

00:53:05.885 --> 00:53:09.360
the most popular book on
administrative segregation,

00:53:09.360 --> 00:53:13.102
which is Chowchilla's version of solitary,

00:53:13.102 --> 00:53:17.610
the most popular book is
"Malice" by Danielle Steele.

00:53:17.610 --> 00:53:21.120
And I said, "That's a novel
about women in prison."

00:53:21.120 --> 00:53:23.220
She goes, "Yeah."

00:53:23.220 --> 00:53:27.750
And I quite ignorantly, this
was several years ago, said,

00:53:27.750 --> 00:53:30.150
and I'm embarrassed to admit
this, but I think it's useful.

00:53:30.150 --> 00:53:32.205
I said, "Why would you wanna read?

00:53:32.205 --> 00:53:34.315
You guys are stuck in prison.

00:53:34.315 --> 00:53:37.324
Why would you wanna read
a novel about prison?"

00:53:37.324 --> 00:53:41.340
And she goes, "Rach, we
don't just wanna read novels

00:53:41.340 --> 00:53:45.120
about your all's divorce or whatever."

00:53:45.120 --> 00:53:49.620
She's like, "We wanna read
books about people like us."

00:53:49.620 --> 00:53:53.040
And what she was doing was challenging me,

00:53:53.040 --> 00:53:55.410
but also supporting me

00:53:55.410 --> 00:53:57.870
because I wanted to write
this novel, "The Mars Room."

00:53:57.870 --> 00:54:01.803
And she was like, you do
that, you write that novel.

00:54:03.139 --> 00:54:06.634
I also, you know, I work
with people in prison

00:54:06.634 --> 00:54:09.602
together on projects.

00:54:09.602 --> 00:54:12.840
I used to be involved with
this organization Justice Now,

00:54:12.840 --> 00:54:15.870
whose board of directors was comprised

00:54:15.870 --> 00:54:18.780
entirely of people inside prison,

00:54:18.780 --> 00:54:21.450
mostly serving very long sentences.

00:54:21.450 --> 00:54:22.950
And the reason that was the case

00:54:22.950 --> 00:54:25.800
was because the woman who
started that organization

00:54:25.800 --> 00:54:28.743
actually got the Fay
Stender Award one year,

00:54:29.640 --> 00:54:31.330
said to a friend of hers inside prison

00:54:31.330 --> 00:54:33.750
bring me like shot callers.

00:54:33.750 --> 00:54:37.860
Bring me the most influential
people in the whole prison.

00:54:37.860 --> 00:54:40.380
And those are people who are
serving very long sentences

00:54:40.380 --> 00:54:42.388
and then they were trained
in human rights work

00:54:42.388 --> 00:54:44.820
to like each one teach one,

00:54:44.820 --> 00:54:48.510
and teach, you know, the
signs of human rights abuses

00:54:48.510 --> 00:54:50.580
to other people in prison.

00:54:50.580 --> 00:54:52.200
Working with those people,

00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:56.490
I am much more a student
than I am a teacher.

00:54:56.490 --> 00:55:01.490
And I don't fool myself that
this is an equitable world.

00:55:02.460 --> 00:55:06.111
I'm acutely aware of
these power differentials.

00:55:06.111 --> 00:55:10.002
They can call me collect, I can
simply not answer the phone.

00:55:10.002 --> 00:55:12.780
I cannot call them, et cetera.

00:55:12.780 --> 00:55:15.960
I think that this work
is uneven and ongoing,

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:16.793
to answer that.

00:55:16.793 --> 00:55:18.960
In terms of, maybe I misunderstand,

00:55:18.960 --> 00:55:20.400
but you brought up morality.

00:55:20.400 --> 00:55:23.400
And I thought this is such
an interesting word to use

00:55:23.400 --> 00:55:27.870
in a world where the religion
and ideology of liberalism

00:55:27.870 --> 00:55:30.930
is that liberalism as
a kind of universalism

00:55:30.930 --> 00:55:34.170
is gonna hold up any person who wants it

00:55:34.170 --> 00:55:36.540
and then can access the kind of freedom

00:55:36.540 --> 00:55:38.340
that liberalism affords us.

00:55:38.340 --> 00:55:40.110
Like consumer freedom.

00:55:40.110 --> 00:55:41.550
This is not the case.

00:55:41.550 --> 00:55:44.700
So then what do we do
with, if I understand it,

00:55:44.700 --> 00:55:49.700
individual morality as opposed
to a collective ethics?

00:55:49.770 --> 00:55:51.030
The last thing I just wanna say quickly

00:55:51.030 --> 00:55:52.950
is listening to Caleb's talk,

00:55:52.950 --> 00:55:56.700
I was reminded of what seems
to me a really terrible irony,

00:55:56.700 --> 00:55:58.410
at least in California,

00:55:58.410 --> 00:56:02.370
which is that maybe the only vestiges left

00:56:02.370 --> 00:56:06.720
of the idea of a fundamentalist
conversion narrative

00:56:06.720 --> 00:56:08.310
in the prisons that are managed

00:56:08.310 --> 00:56:10.260
by the California
Department of Corrections

00:56:10.260 --> 00:56:13.590
is that the Bible is the
one thing you can have

00:56:13.590 --> 00:56:15.630
no matter what you've done in prison.

00:56:15.630 --> 00:56:18.150
When you find yourself
in the jail of the prison

00:56:18.150 --> 00:56:20.400
or the jail of the jail of the prison,

00:56:20.400 --> 00:56:23.040
which would be to go to solitary

00:56:23.040 --> 00:56:24.720
and leave your prison, that ad-seg,

00:56:24.720 --> 00:56:26.893
and go to an ad-seg ad-seg prison,

00:56:26.893 --> 00:56:29.850
they will never take away your Bible.

00:56:29.850 --> 00:56:31.737
And it's almost as if they are saying,

00:56:31.737 --> 00:56:34.590
and I only realize this
listening to him talk,

00:56:34.590 --> 00:56:38.640
that those inside prison
who have messed up

00:56:38.640 --> 00:56:40.382
are condemned to religion

00:56:40.382 --> 00:56:42.570
in a way that the rest of us are not.

00:56:42.570 --> 00:56:45.960
We can continue to shop and
lease cars and do this and that,

00:56:45.960 --> 00:56:47.283
but they have to pray.

00:56:51.390 --> 00:56:55.563
- What you're describing is the ongoing.

00:56:56.640 --> 00:57:00.909
It's exactly continuous with
evangelical prison reform

00:57:00.909 --> 00:57:05.490
in the 1820s whose project
was to mass print Bibles

00:57:05.490 --> 00:57:07.530
and put one in every single cell.

00:57:07.530 --> 00:57:10.470
When Jacob Hodges was released,

00:57:10.470 --> 00:57:11.970
one of the reformers went and asked him

00:57:11.970 --> 00:57:14.310
if he could have the prison
Bible that had converted,

00:57:14.310 --> 00:57:15.853
that Hodges had had his conversion with.

00:57:15.853 --> 00:57:18.810
And Hodges was like,
"No, you can't have it.

00:57:18.810 --> 00:57:20.617
Why would you presume that
you can take this away from,

00:57:20.617 --> 00:57:22.320
you know, this one thing?"

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:23.340
And after he died,

00:57:23.340 --> 00:57:25.530
Hodges sent it to the Reform Society

00:57:25.530 --> 00:57:27.570
and this minister, Louis Dwight,

00:57:27.570 --> 00:57:29.730
used to go around holding up Hodges' Bible

00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:33.030
as a kind of prop in his sermons.

00:57:33.030 --> 00:57:34.646
It's an interesting thing
because Evangelicalism,

00:57:34.646 --> 00:57:38.100
it was like the first mass
media religious movement

00:57:38.100 --> 00:57:41.011
just as it has become like a televangelism

00:57:41.011 --> 00:57:43.680
and internet phenomenon now.

00:57:43.680 --> 00:57:46.521
But suddenly the mass printing gave,

00:57:46.521 --> 00:57:49.830
like regressed back to
this fetish of the single,

00:57:49.830 --> 00:57:52.306
the single object that
had converted Hodges.

00:57:52.306 --> 00:57:53.250
(panelist speaking off mic)

00:57:53.250 --> 00:57:55.890
Yeah, the aura comes back, yeah.

00:57:55.890 --> 00:57:57.035
I wanted to say a little bit about

00:57:57.035 --> 00:58:01.740
to Joy's great question
about what forms of community

00:58:01.740 --> 00:58:05.733
are kind of broken
apart and reconstituted.

00:58:07.410 --> 00:58:11.420
Sometimes when we describe criminalization

00:58:11.420 --> 00:58:16.420
and incarceration as a tactic
of class war and race war,

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:18.930
the response to that is, oh,

00:58:18.930 --> 00:58:21.240
but a lot of poor people
support harsh punishment.

00:58:21.240 --> 00:58:23.550
Or a lot of people in the
Black community support,

00:58:23.550 --> 00:58:25.320
want police to keep
them safe or something.

00:58:25.320 --> 00:58:30.074
And the point is not people
with privilege and property

00:58:30.074 --> 00:58:33.540
support harsh punishment
and everyone else doesn't.

00:58:33.540 --> 00:58:36.360
It's that criminalization
and incarceration

00:58:36.360 --> 00:58:38.970
and policing as the
only point of interface

00:58:38.970 --> 00:58:43.200
with any kind of state
service divides, right?

00:58:43.200 --> 00:58:44.550
It creates this division.

00:58:44.550 --> 00:58:47.416
And in New York state
already in the 1840s,

00:58:47.416 --> 00:58:50.700
the stone cutting masons at large

00:58:50.700 --> 00:58:51.617
were saying we can't compete

00:58:51.617 --> 00:58:53.490
with this incarcerated labor.

00:58:53.490 --> 00:58:56.041
That's no-wage labor, and
it's creating a class division

00:58:56.041 --> 00:58:59.457
within this emergent working
class in the era of wage labor,

00:58:59.457 --> 00:59:02.640
and that is ongoing.

00:59:02.640 --> 00:59:03.473
But it's also true that we see,

00:59:03.473 --> 00:59:06.402
I mean, I think your point
about some conversions

00:59:06.402 --> 00:59:08.436
are isolating and some are conversions

00:59:08.436 --> 00:59:11.520
into a different solidarity
or into a different community

00:59:11.520 --> 00:59:13.050
is really a meaningful one

00:59:13.050 --> 00:59:15.573
that I didn't draw out
much in my presentation.

00:59:17.220 --> 00:59:18.990
You know, often those of us who teach

00:59:18.990 --> 00:59:19.823
learn from our students

00:59:19.823 --> 00:59:21.372
that they've been going off to the block

00:59:21.372 --> 00:59:24.930
and conducting seminars
on the same topics.

00:59:24.930 --> 00:59:27.570
Not that they're transmitting
what I said about the text,

00:59:27.570 --> 00:59:29.820
but that they're having their
own discussion about the text.

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:31.080
And some of that is coming back

00:59:31.080 --> 00:59:33.033
to change our conversation in the room.

00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:37.983
The kinds of,

00:59:39.297 --> 00:59:44.297
the the kinds of small scale
connections and community

00:59:44.460 --> 00:59:47.100
that get formed on those
long bus rides, you know,

00:59:47.100 --> 00:59:48.720
out to the Central Valley or something

00:59:48.720 --> 00:59:50.970
and then become networks of activism

00:59:50.970 --> 00:59:54.453
that can link out and so on.

00:59:55.650 --> 00:59:58.560
I think, and I really like the distinction

00:59:58.560 --> 01:00:02.400
between a kind of moralism and an ethics.

01:00:02.400 --> 01:00:05.250
For my part, I do become a little wary

01:00:05.250 --> 01:00:09.603
when I hear a language of we
need a reckoning on this topic,

01:00:11.040 --> 01:00:13.110
as if a kind, you know,

01:00:13.110 --> 01:00:15.627
as if the spirit is gonna
sweep across the land

01:00:15.627 --> 01:00:18.693
and people are gonna embrace
a new faith on justice.

01:00:20.670 --> 01:00:23.833
To me, that is, that kind of talk is,

01:00:27.240 --> 01:00:30.802
expresses in part a sense of dispossession

01:00:30.802 --> 01:00:33.090
and no access to the real mechanisms

01:00:33.090 --> 01:00:35.850
of how you change a political structure.

01:00:35.850 --> 01:00:38.670
And so the idea of an
ethic that's more about,

01:00:38.670 --> 01:00:41.373
well, people might
actually have many reasons

01:00:41.373 --> 01:00:43.290
for being against this

01:00:43.290 --> 01:00:45.160
or for being in solidarity
with each other,

01:00:45.160 --> 01:00:48.480
and that there would be
a kind of lateral ethic

01:00:48.480 --> 01:00:50.790
of mutual respect of what
brings people to the work

01:00:50.790 --> 01:00:53.013
around a common project is the way that I,

01:00:54.628 --> 01:00:56.697
that I would understand that difference

01:00:56.697 --> 01:01:00.210
between a kind of, you know,

01:01:00.210 --> 01:01:05.210
an awakening and a kind
of solidarity ethic.

01:01:05.730 --> 01:01:06.830
You know this...

01:01:09.897 --> 01:01:11.464
Well, I think, yeah, I
think that's what, that's...

01:01:11.464 --> 01:01:14.340
I wanna give other
people some time to talk.

01:01:14.340 --> 01:01:19.143
- So this is a half joke for
Rachel, as a former seminarian.

01:01:20.250 --> 01:01:21.450
I'm praying it works,

01:01:21.450 --> 01:01:24.540
but also I think it's interesting,

01:01:24.540 --> 01:01:26.580
we've talked a lot about Christianity.

01:01:26.580 --> 01:01:28.920
So a number of people that I know who...

01:01:28.920 --> 01:01:31.980
Like so my work right, is political theory

01:01:31.980 --> 01:01:35.640
and I work with radical,
you know, theorists,

01:01:35.640 --> 01:01:40.120
but people who were in the
Panthers are now Muslims.

01:01:40.980 --> 01:01:42.480
So their names are changed,

01:01:42.480 --> 01:01:46.162
Dhoruba bin Wahad, Jalil
Muntaqim, Assata Shakur, right?

01:01:46.162 --> 01:01:49.590
So there is something about
spirituality and religion,

01:01:49.590 --> 01:01:52.740
and maybe because it is Islam that is,

01:01:52.740 --> 01:01:55.140
it's also a counter to, you know,

01:01:55.140 --> 01:01:57.207
a reactionary Christian state

01:01:57.207 --> 01:02:00.660
or certain kind of dictates,

01:02:00.660 --> 01:02:02.460
like you're saying the Bible is sacred.

01:02:02.460 --> 01:02:03.720
You could, you know, that's your key.

01:02:03.720 --> 01:02:05.460
You could carry it anywhere.

01:02:05.460 --> 01:02:07.110
Quran maybe no.

01:02:07.110 --> 01:02:08.640
If you're a Buddhist, you know,

01:02:08.640 --> 01:02:10.593
maybe you have a little gray area.

01:02:11.640 --> 01:02:14.040
I'm wondering to go back
to something I asked before

01:02:14.040 --> 01:02:15.740
and I also wanna put on the table,

01:02:16.920 --> 01:02:21.480
there's a prison strike
right now in Alabama, right?

01:02:21.480 --> 01:02:23.673
And so we talk a lot about censorship

01:02:23.673 --> 01:02:25.500
around critical race theory.

01:02:25.500 --> 01:02:28.409
Like the banned books thing
that appears to be mushrooming,

01:02:28.409 --> 01:02:33.409
you know, from the South and
then moving across the US.

01:02:34.115 --> 01:02:36.960
I'm wondering if you have any thoughts

01:02:36.960 --> 01:02:41.520
about how incarcerated
writers are censored

01:02:41.520 --> 01:02:44.940
and how it's difficult for
them to get their works out,

01:02:44.940 --> 01:02:48.690
particularly if they're
critical of the state itself?

01:02:48.690 --> 01:02:51.690
'Cause we've never, we know that the state

01:02:51.690 --> 01:02:55.470
has predatory strategies and a history.

01:02:55.470 --> 01:03:00.340
The 13th Amendment legalizes
enslavement in prisons

01:03:00.340 --> 01:03:03.330
or if you've been duly
convicted of a crime.

01:03:03.330 --> 01:03:05.160
So that's kind of on the table.

01:03:05.160 --> 01:03:07.440
And then the last thing,
and then I'll stop,

01:03:07.440 --> 01:03:10.090
is the dichotomy between the privileged

01:03:11.370 --> 01:03:12.930
imprisoned or prisoner

01:03:12.930 --> 01:03:16.590
and then the one that is expendable.

01:03:16.590 --> 01:03:18.330
So when Davis was on trial,

01:03:18.330 --> 01:03:20.700
she had a co-defendant, Ruchell Magee,

01:03:20.700 --> 01:03:24.090
and there was an online
celebration of his 82nd birthday.

01:03:24.090 --> 01:03:26.400
He's still inside, right?

01:03:26.400 --> 01:03:28.260
Because he didn't, he, you know,

01:03:28.260 --> 01:03:29.813
one, he actually was in Marin County

01:03:29.813 --> 01:03:32.684
and he was holding one of her guns, right?

01:03:32.684 --> 01:03:36.642
But his argument is that he was a slave,

01:03:36.642 --> 01:03:40.770
and all slaves have the
right to fugitivity.

01:03:40.770 --> 01:03:43.800
Davis' position was
that she is not a slave,

01:03:43.800 --> 01:03:45.480
she's a citizen.

01:03:45.480 --> 01:03:48.300
And so she has the right
to the protection of law.

01:03:48.300 --> 01:03:51.000
There are segments of the incarcerated

01:03:51.000 --> 01:03:54.370
who see themselves as captives

01:03:55.230 --> 01:03:57.510
through the filter of the 13th Amendment.

01:03:57.510 --> 01:03:58.980
And so their writings will be

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:02.160
less likely to appear in public.

01:04:02.160 --> 01:04:05.613
And so the public's
ability to protect them,

01:04:06.510 --> 01:04:09.213
if, you know, like Stevie Wilson
who's a Black queer writer,

01:04:09.213 --> 01:04:12.360
he's continuously thrown in the hole

01:04:12.360 --> 01:04:15.270
because he's talking
about abuses by guards,

01:04:15.270 --> 01:04:16.539
abuses by systems, right?

01:04:16.539 --> 01:04:18.810
So I'm wondering about, you know,

01:04:18.810 --> 01:04:23.310
the protective mechanism
that the author can offer

01:04:24.498 --> 01:04:26.043
to the incarcerated?

01:04:27.510 --> 01:04:28.343
- [Rachel] Can I?

01:04:28.343 --> 01:04:29.176
- [Caleb] Yeah.

01:04:30.300 --> 01:04:32.850
- Man, I mean we could
just, I wish, you know,

01:04:32.850 --> 01:04:33.683
we don't have that much time,

01:04:33.683 --> 01:04:36.030
and then you just raised
many important points,

01:04:36.030 --> 01:04:38.881
but just a couple things
that come to mind.

01:04:38.881 --> 01:04:40.500
(clearing throat)

01:04:40.500 --> 01:04:43.221
Sorry to sound acerbic,
but the Marshall Project

01:04:43.221 --> 01:04:47.010
is an organization that
was started by Bill Keller,

01:04:47.010 --> 01:04:50.169
who used to be, was the editor
of "The New York Times."

01:04:50.169 --> 01:04:53.221
And it's meant to be a project

01:04:53.221 --> 01:04:55.805
about incarceration in America.

01:04:55.805 --> 01:04:57.480
And when it was first started,

01:04:57.480 --> 01:04:59.520
they had a kind of like
fundraising cocktail party

01:04:59.520 --> 01:05:01.290
in Los Angeles and somebody invited me

01:05:01.290 --> 01:05:03.540
even though I was not the
target audience of the party,

01:05:03.540 --> 01:05:05.280
which was about raising money.

01:05:05.280 --> 01:05:09.810
And I asked, "Are you
going to have a section

01:05:09.810 --> 01:05:12.471
where you share the writing
of incarcerated people?"

01:05:12.471 --> 01:05:15.690
And Bill Keller said,
"Well, this is not gonna be

01:05:15.690 --> 01:05:17.070
prison poetry monthly."

01:05:17.070 --> 01:05:18.587
And people went, "Ha, ha, ha, ha."

01:05:18.587 --> 01:05:22.079
And I, you know, wanted to
gun down the entire crowd.

01:05:22.079 --> 01:05:24.744
But so then the Marshall Project started,

01:05:24.744 --> 01:05:27.313
and I did turn to it as a resource

01:05:27.313 --> 01:05:29.040
for friends inside prison

01:05:29.040 --> 01:05:31.410
who wanted an outlet for their writing

01:05:31.410 --> 01:05:32.490
because of what they were doing

01:05:32.490 --> 01:05:34.710
and they had a really,
you know, a big platform

01:05:34.710 --> 01:05:36.120
and a lot of money behind it.

01:05:36.120 --> 01:05:39.240
And one friend of mine's
work was published

01:05:39.240 --> 01:05:40.676
in the Marshall Project twice,

01:05:40.676 --> 01:05:45.300
and I had to be her interlocutor with them

01:05:45.300 --> 01:05:48.180
because they didn't have a
JPay thing set up at the time.

01:05:48.180 --> 01:05:49.470
And so she would send the pieces to me

01:05:49.470 --> 01:05:50.700
and I would send them to them.

01:05:50.700 --> 01:05:54.189
And I noticed that she was
always calling the editor Mister.

01:05:54.189 --> 01:05:56.017
And I would have these
conversations with her,

01:05:56.017 --> 01:06:00.120
"Don't call him Mr., you
know, he's merely your editor.

01:06:00.120 --> 01:06:01.320
He's not a guard.

01:06:01.320 --> 01:06:03.420
He is not your counselor in prison."

01:06:03.420 --> 01:06:04.924
But it was just so hard for her

01:06:04.924 --> 01:06:07.890
to get beyond these kinds of institutional

01:06:07.890 --> 01:06:11.250
hierarchized sorts of exchanges.

01:06:11.250 --> 01:06:14.070
Anyone free is like a Mr. or a Mrs.

01:06:14.070 --> 01:06:16.830
So when her piece, her
first piece was published,

01:06:16.830 --> 01:06:18.780
for the biography, the editor asked me,

01:06:18.780 --> 01:06:19.920
can you supply a bio?

01:06:19.920 --> 01:06:22.980
No, he didn't ask me can
you supply a biography?

01:06:22.980 --> 01:06:24.660
He didn't say anything about the bio.

01:06:24.660 --> 01:06:27.720
And I said, "Do you need
a bio for this writer?"

01:06:27.720 --> 01:06:29.665
And he said, "Oh, for
the incarcerated writers,

01:06:29.665 --> 01:06:34.665
we include their age,

01:06:35.310 --> 01:06:37.260
the prison where they're incarcerated,

01:06:37.260 --> 01:06:41.487
and the crime of which
they were convicted."

01:06:42.510 --> 01:06:47.510
And I honestly saw a man
opening a woman's mouth

01:06:48.510 --> 01:06:50.340
to count her teeth.

01:06:50.340 --> 01:06:52.751
And so I just totally
freaked out and said,

01:06:52.751 --> 01:06:54.052
you can't, you know?

01:06:54.052 --> 01:06:55.830
But then I'm in this position,

01:06:55.830 --> 01:06:57.990
do I wanna stop my friend

01:06:57.990 --> 01:06:59.479
from being published in this magazine

01:06:59.479 --> 01:07:02.520
or do I have to assent to their rules?

01:07:02.520 --> 01:07:05.040
And I got into a big fight
with the Marshall Project

01:07:05.040 --> 01:07:06.300
that is ongoing to this day,

01:07:06.300 --> 01:07:07.657
but for that one thing he said,

01:07:07.657 --> 01:07:10.710
"I just won't include a bio."

01:07:10.710 --> 01:07:12.720
So she didn't get to be treated

01:07:12.720 --> 01:07:15.570
with the normal courtesy with
which a writer was treated.

01:07:15.570 --> 01:07:18.600
And at first I tried to use
kind of obfuscation and say,

01:07:18.600 --> 01:07:20.911
oh, women don't like to
have their age mentioned

01:07:20.911 --> 01:07:22.020
in their bio.

01:07:22.020 --> 01:07:24.570
That's not the normal kind
of thing you would put.

01:07:24.570 --> 01:07:29.570
I mean, it was like birthdate,
location, and crime.

01:07:30.101 --> 01:07:33.649
So there are these outlets
that we're given as resources

01:07:33.649 --> 01:07:36.060
to help our friends inside prison,

01:07:36.060 --> 01:07:39.720
but we have to push
back against these idea

01:07:39.720 --> 01:07:42.360
that they would be otherized
at every step of the way.

01:07:42.360 --> 01:07:44.580
I don't know if that speaks
to some of what you're saying,

01:07:44.580 --> 01:07:46.200
but that's what came to mind.

01:07:46.200 --> 01:07:47.820
- I just have a short update

01:07:47.820 --> 01:07:50.130
'cause I was talking with John Lennon

01:07:50.130 --> 01:07:51.780
about this very issue recently.

01:07:51.780 --> 01:07:54.120
He's been, he has been a contributor

01:07:54.120 --> 01:07:55.020
to the Marshall Project,

01:07:55.020 --> 01:07:59.127
and your advocacy, I think,
had some effect, Rachel,

01:07:59.127 --> 01:08:02.430
although I think he and other
people have continued it.

01:08:02.430 --> 01:08:05.340
So he wound up as an
advisor to what is, I think,

01:08:05.340 --> 01:08:07.050
and I don't work at the Marshall Project,

01:08:07.050 --> 01:08:09.420
so if other people have other information

01:08:09.420 --> 01:08:11.370
maybe we can add that
during the questions.

01:08:11.370 --> 01:08:13.590
But he became an advisor to what I think

01:08:13.590 --> 01:08:18.030
has become a sort of regular
contributor feature part

01:08:18.030 --> 01:08:19.020
of the publication,

01:08:19.020 --> 01:08:21.570
which is voices from
people who are incarcerated

01:08:21.570 --> 01:08:23.943
and submissions and
essays they've written.

01:08:25.050 --> 01:08:27.480
And he was saying that his battle

01:08:27.480 --> 01:08:30.090
was to get those names onto the masthead

01:08:30.090 --> 01:08:32.320
so that there would be
this kind of, you know,

01:08:32.320 --> 01:08:35.100
real recognition of the
contributions they were making.

01:08:35.100 --> 01:08:36.802
Anyway, I just had to
add that little update.

01:08:36.802 --> 01:08:37.635
- [Rachel] No, please.

01:08:37.635 --> 01:08:39.120
- It's really interesting.

01:08:39.120 --> 01:08:41.520
And, you know, reducing people to their,

01:08:41.520 --> 01:08:44.370
especially deciding that the
crime of their conviction

01:08:44.370 --> 01:08:46.708
has to be the kind of tagline

01:08:46.708 --> 01:08:50.070
when that's not something
they're choosing is,

01:08:50.070 --> 01:08:52.980
yeah, kind of amazing,
and I guess also not.

01:08:52.980 --> 01:08:53.813
Caleb?

01:08:53.813 --> 01:08:57.390
- Yeah, well, I wanted to
give at least a mention

01:08:57.390 --> 01:09:00.120
to Joy's volume, "The New Abolitionist,"

01:09:00.120 --> 01:09:04.140
which is, has this work
that she's describing,

01:09:04.140 --> 01:09:09.140
and encourage people to read
the writers that are in that.

01:09:09.360 --> 01:09:11.776
I also wanna tell a little
bit about my experience

01:09:11.776 --> 01:09:16.776
in discussing the 1858 prison memoir,

01:09:16.820 --> 01:09:19.980
"The Life and the Adventures
of a Haunted Convict,"

01:09:19.980 --> 01:09:22.830
which Joy wrote an incredible essay about

01:09:22.830 --> 01:09:23.730
for "Boston Review."

01:09:23.730 --> 01:09:27.360
That's kind of how we
first got to be in touch.

01:09:27.360 --> 01:09:32.360
But Reed wrote a retrospective memoir

01:09:33.840 --> 01:09:36.330
of his life in the 1850s.

01:09:36.330 --> 01:09:41.250
And as I traveled around with that

01:09:41.250 --> 01:09:44.160
and describing his life
and the conditions,

01:09:44.160 --> 01:09:45.720
he was also at Auburn State Prison

01:09:45.720 --> 01:09:46.920
that I've just been describing,

01:09:46.920 --> 01:09:51.920
which was the birthplace of
the sort of penal servitude

01:09:54.750 --> 01:09:58.110
that did eventually get
enshrined in the 13th Amendment.

01:09:58.110 --> 01:10:00.380
That is, it was Northern
industrial penal servitude

01:10:00.380 --> 01:10:03.194
that was for which an exception was made

01:10:03.194 --> 01:10:05.070
that then came to be exploited

01:10:05.070 --> 01:10:08.490
by the Southern states in
that re-enslavement project

01:10:08.490 --> 01:10:10.017
because Auburn could not have operated

01:10:10.017 --> 01:10:14.403
without this unwaged
labor in its workshops.

01:10:15.330 --> 01:10:16.680
Anyway, the people,

01:10:16.680 --> 01:10:19.287
as I discussed Reed and
his life story, people,

01:10:19.287 --> 01:10:21.687
this goes back also the
question of genre or fiction

01:10:21.687 --> 01:10:24.480
and nonfiction kinds of narrative.

01:10:24.480 --> 01:10:29.480
The crowds were very deeply
preset with two expectations.

01:10:30.490 --> 01:10:33.250
One: That his book was a diary or journal

01:10:34.320 --> 01:10:38.190
rather than a retrospectively
crafted narrative of his life.

01:10:38.190 --> 01:10:41.382
So they wanted the kind of
access to the raw experience

01:10:41.382 --> 01:10:45.690
unmanipulated by art somehow.

01:10:45.690 --> 01:10:47.190
And two: That he was innocent.

01:10:48.660 --> 01:10:50.070
Neither of which was the case.

01:10:50.070 --> 01:10:54.310
He fully, he described his
participation in crimes

01:10:55.485 --> 01:10:57.900
and never, I mean,

01:10:57.900 --> 01:10:59.370
he had a kind of protest

01:10:59.370 --> 01:11:01.857
against the entire system,

01:11:01.857 --> 01:11:04.650
but on the specific grounds
of his own, you know,

01:11:04.650 --> 01:11:06.744
of the accusation and sentencing

01:11:06.744 --> 01:11:09.333
there wasn't a disagreement.

01:11:11.130 --> 01:11:13.740
And so that does seem to me

01:11:13.740 --> 01:11:15.540
to speak to what you're saying

01:11:15.540 --> 01:11:18.250
about what kinds of stories people expect

01:11:18.250 --> 01:11:20.220
and are ready to endorse.

01:11:20.220 --> 01:11:22.740
And this question of innocence
that you raised before

01:11:22.740 --> 01:11:26.760
and what kinds they don't want to hear

01:11:26.760 --> 01:11:29.670
or they don't want to think
of as being, you know,

01:11:29.670 --> 01:11:31.863
on their side or politically aligned with.

01:11:34.170 --> 01:11:36.650
I think that it also...

01:11:38.220 --> 01:11:40.569
And so there are truths
that people don't want told

01:11:40.569 --> 01:11:42.243
in this writing.

01:11:43.470 --> 01:11:47.550
But the thing about the
wanting it to be a diary

01:11:47.550 --> 01:11:52.500
also suggests another kind of
pressure that's alongside that

01:11:52.500 --> 01:11:53.640
in some awkward ways,

01:11:53.640 --> 01:11:57.271
which is that incarcerated
writers are only

01:11:57.271 --> 01:12:00.903
supposed to talk about their
own experience as such.

01:12:03.480 --> 01:12:05.460
And so there's a kind of like

01:12:05.460 --> 01:12:06.390
we're not coming to them

01:12:06.390 --> 01:12:08.070
for their poetry or whatever, right?

01:12:08.070 --> 01:12:10.936
There's the this isn't,

01:12:10.936 --> 01:12:12.990
I don't want your artistry, you know?

01:12:12.990 --> 01:12:17.490
I want the kind of auto
ethnographic truth of this.

01:12:17.490 --> 01:12:19.978
And Reed has an amazing moment in his book

01:12:19.978 --> 01:12:22.086
where he talks about being asked

01:12:22.086 --> 01:12:27.086
by a group of kind of anti-slavery,
wealthy White liberals

01:12:27.515 --> 01:12:32.515
to recite some abolitionist
poetry for them, which he does.

01:12:32.610 --> 01:12:34.830
And they're moved to tears.

01:12:34.830 --> 01:12:37.020
He recites a poem by the British

01:12:37.020 --> 01:12:38.943
anti-slavery poet, William Cooper.

01:12:40.140 --> 01:12:43.590
They give him money, they
offer to support his education.

01:12:43.590 --> 01:12:46.357
And then a kind of aside
to the reader, he says,

01:12:46.357 --> 01:12:47.333
"I wonder what they would've said

01:12:47.333 --> 01:12:50.067
if I recited some outlaw ballads instead?"

01:12:51.720 --> 01:12:56.100
He's very sophisticated
about what kind of sympathy

01:12:56.100 --> 01:12:57.363
is available to him,

01:12:58.911 --> 01:13:03.570
what kind of charity case
he's being asked to play

01:13:03.570 --> 01:13:05.010
in that situation,

01:13:05.010 --> 01:13:07.873
and what the kind of like
White racial liberalism

01:13:07.873 --> 01:13:09.687
of that economy is.

01:13:09.687 --> 01:13:14.687
And that he carves out
for him space for himself.

01:13:16.800 --> 01:13:18.240
To think of himself as someone

01:13:18.240 --> 01:13:19.590
who could deploy many genres,

01:13:19.590 --> 01:13:22.140
as an artist who's capable
of many kinds of storytelling

01:13:22.140 --> 01:13:23.880
and who could confront them with something

01:13:23.880 --> 01:13:27.363
that wouldn't be so readily acceptable.

01:13:30.960 --> 01:13:33.990
You know, as a scholar of 19th century,

01:13:33.990 --> 01:13:35.280
I'm routing a lot of my answers

01:13:35.280 --> 01:13:37.200
back through this historical stuff,

01:13:37.200 --> 01:13:40.496
but I do think that there
is some continuity there.

01:13:40.496 --> 01:13:45.496
I would say there's this double
bind of tell us the truth,

01:13:46.380 --> 01:13:48.543
but not too much, right?

01:13:50.760 --> 01:13:52.680
- And I think, Joy, that
speaks to the question

01:13:52.680 --> 01:13:54.990
you were asking about prisoners

01:13:54.990 --> 01:13:59.670
who are writing a more 13th
Amendment-based challenge

01:13:59.670 --> 01:14:01.441
to their incarceration.

01:14:01.441 --> 01:14:06.441
And I wonder if that for you
brings up any other questions

01:14:06.533 --> 01:14:10.320
about just like the limits of
this kind of communication?

01:14:10.320 --> 01:14:14.187
I mean, are we, we as
a, obviously, you know,

01:14:14.187 --> 01:14:16.222
the people who are in charge
of the prison environment

01:14:16.222 --> 01:14:17.734
have an enormous amount of control

01:14:17.734 --> 01:14:19.706
over what comes out

01:14:19.706 --> 01:14:24.706
unless people are able to
smuggle images and writings out.

01:14:25.200 --> 01:14:27.040
And if you wanna put
your name on something

01:14:27.040 --> 01:14:30.175
that you're writing, then the, you know,

01:14:30.175 --> 01:14:35.175
the smuggling avenue is you can do it,

01:14:36.101 --> 01:14:38.520
but then you're gonna be
punished for it, right?

01:14:38.520 --> 01:14:40.443
And so we're, there,

01:14:41.280 --> 01:14:43.800
so I guess I'm wondering about two things.

01:14:43.800 --> 01:14:48.060
So there is this real
cost that someone like,

01:14:48.060 --> 01:14:49.080
sorry, I've forgotten the name

01:14:49.080 --> 01:14:51.090
of the person you were referencing before

01:14:51.090 --> 01:14:53.730
who was not able to get his writings out

01:14:53.730 --> 01:14:54.930
or who's being put into--

01:14:54.930 --> 01:14:55.763
- [Joy] Stevie Wilson.

01:14:55.763 --> 01:14:56.747
- Stevie Wilson?

01:14:56.747 --> 01:14:58.440
- [Joy] Some of his work is out,

01:14:58.440 --> 01:15:00.300
but he's continuously harassed.

01:15:00.300 --> 01:15:03.119
- Yes, so he's having
this particular dilemma

01:15:03.119 --> 01:15:05.460
of wanting to be the named author

01:15:05.460 --> 01:15:06.360
in the work he's producing

01:15:06.360 --> 01:15:09.270
because it's his creative
mind that's producing it.

01:15:09.270 --> 01:15:10.740
It's his work.

01:15:10.740 --> 01:15:13.200
It should have that recognition.

01:15:13.200 --> 01:15:16.650
But there's this incredibly
clear cost to bear.

01:15:16.650 --> 01:15:20.491
So that's one sort of very
contemporary manifestation,

01:15:20.491 --> 01:15:23.850
I think, of the limitations
that Caleb was talking about.

01:15:23.850 --> 01:15:26.820
And then we are having this moment

01:15:26.820 --> 01:15:28.227
where prisons are losing control

01:15:28.227 --> 01:15:30.876
over some of the particularly photographs

01:15:30.876 --> 01:15:33.806
and even videos that are coming
out of these environments

01:15:33.806 --> 01:15:36.930
because of the, you
know, cell phone culture

01:15:36.930 --> 01:15:39.780
in which people are able
to document their worlds.

01:15:39.780 --> 01:15:42.517
And in the last several months,

01:15:42.517 --> 01:15:44.064
"The New York Times" other publications

01:15:44.064 --> 01:15:48.120
have run these incredibly
disturbing set of photos

01:15:48.120 --> 01:15:49.890
and images that, you know,

01:15:49.890 --> 01:15:52.410
people within the walls took to document

01:15:52.410 --> 01:15:53.537
and show that they were happening.

01:15:53.537 --> 01:15:57.000
And the reason those images can come out

01:15:57.000 --> 01:15:59.250
with maybe a little more safety

01:15:59.250 --> 01:16:00.155
for the people producing them

01:16:00.155 --> 01:16:01.710
is that they're anonymous.

01:16:01.710 --> 01:16:04.227
And so their power is, doesn't,

01:16:04.227 --> 01:16:07.924
you don't get to claim the
ownership and authorship of it.

01:16:07.924 --> 01:16:10.036
Anyway, I just was
thinking about those things

01:16:10.036 --> 01:16:11.070
as you were talking.

01:16:11.070 --> 01:16:12.586
- Yeah, so two quick things.

01:16:12.586 --> 01:16:16.300
The reason why, Caleb, I
got into anthologizing,

01:16:16.300 --> 01:16:18.079
I mean, I said it elsewhere,

01:16:18.079 --> 01:16:22.560
is that I was asked by
Davis to do a prototype

01:16:22.560 --> 01:16:23.820
for critical resistance,

01:16:23.820 --> 01:16:25.980
and I did at CU Boulder, Colorado,

01:16:25.980 --> 01:16:28.650
and then anthologized, you know,

01:16:28.650 --> 01:16:31.773
the academics and the
intellectuals who were there.

01:16:31.773 --> 01:16:34.511
And I agreed with the
press or asked the press

01:16:34.511 --> 01:16:37.380
to send 50 copies inside.

01:16:37.380 --> 01:16:39.750
And so I was given a list
of incarcerated people,

01:16:39.750 --> 01:16:42.600
including people who'd
been in the Panther Party.

01:16:42.600 --> 01:16:47.250
And so one person who'd been
in the Party wrote back.

01:16:47.250 --> 01:16:50.250
Again, I say, you know,
it's kind of a joke on me.

01:16:50.250 --> 01:16:51.312
You know, I thought I was being helpful.

01:16:51.312 --> 01:16:54.540
That the book wasn't, he
didn't say it wasn't that good,

01:16:54.540 --> 01:16:55.410
he just said thank you.

01:16:55.410 --> 01:16:59.260
But you know, it doesn't really reflect

01:17:00.120 --> 01:17:03.420
the conditions inside
of long-term detainees,

01:17:03.420 --> 01:17:05.640
particularly those who
came in as political

01:17:05.640 --> 01:17:08.040
because they have surplus torture.

01:17:08.040 --> 01:17:10.103
'Cause the point is like we
don't just wanna break you.

01:17:10.103 --> 01:17:13.917
We wanna break your
connection to these ideas

01:17:13.917 --> 01:17:17.730
that make you think you could
take on the state, right?

01:17:17.730 --> 01:17:20.343
And so when I went to Brown from there,

01:17:20.343 --> 01:17:23.836
all I did was anthologize, right?

01:17:23.836 --> 01:17:27.990
But also people weren't ready
for it so I left eventually.

01:17:27.990 --> 01:17:28.841
But when I was writing,

01:17:28.841 --> 01:17:30.930
it could be the Barrigans who were,

01:17:30.930 --> 01:17:33.077
you know, pacifists.

01:17:33.077 --> 01:17:35.550
Like Daniel, I can't remember.

01:17:35.550 --> 01:17:37.860
One of them had cancer and died inside.

01:17:37.860 --> 01:17:40.470
So I think it was Philip or Daniel.

01:17:40.470 --> 01:17:42.270
I'm sorry, I keep mixing up the brothers.

01:17:42.270 --> 01:17:44.970
They're Catholics who were
against the war in Vietnam.

01:17:44.970 --> 01:17:46.050
They were Catholic priests,

01:17:46.050 --> 01:17:49.740
and then one of them
left the church, right?

01:17:49.740 --> 01:17:51.340
He was a contributor, right?

01:17:51.340 --> 01:17:54.398
And but then you get letters
back, so the consequences.

01:17:54.398 --> 01:17:57.930
You get the letters back
from the prison officials

01:17:57.930 --> 01:18:01.950
that you're trending
towards in legal behavior

01:18:01.950 --> 01:18:05.443
by trying to get information
from the incarcerated

01:18:05.443 --> 01:18:08.310
about their perspective and politics.

01:18:08.310 --> 01:18:10.897
You get pushback from
the university itself

01:18:10.897 --> 01:18:13.419
'cause they're not ready for conferences

01:18:13.419 --> 01:18:16.410
where former like Weather
Underground, you know,

01:18:16.410 --> 01:18:17.520
Laura Whitehorn or something

01:18:17.520 --> 01:18:18.840
come to campus and speak.

01:18:18.840 --> 01:18:21.660
Like that was prohibited 20 years ago.

01:18:21.660 --> 01:18:23.310
Now it's okay.

01:18:23.310 --> 01:18:25.410
So then one of the things
that comes into my mind,

01:18:25.410 --> 01:18:27.570
I mean, maybe like I should take more naps

01:18:27.570 --> 01:18:28.525
and not think this much,

01:18:28.525 --> 01:18:32.160
but it seems like what you couldn't do

01:18:32.160 --> 01:18:35.010
20 years ago with rebels, you can do now.

01:18:35.010 --> 01:18:36.753
I have former students.

01:18:38.460 --> 01:18:40.890
When I was at Brown I was
teaching courses on the Panthers

01:18:40.890 --> 01:18:42.583
who, you know, they're writers now.

01:18:42.583 --> 01:18:46.350
They're doing a book or
other people are doing books

01:18:46.350 --> 01:18:47.755
on George Jackson.

01:18:47.755 --> 01:18:51.780
I could barely teach
George Jackson at Brown.

01:18:51.780 --> 01:18:54.150
I mean my students went
on a verbal strike.

01:18:54.150 --> 01:18:57.378
They sat in the class for two
hours and didn't say anything

01:18:57.378 --> 01:19:00.150
'cause I assigned George
Jackson's "Soledad Brother"

01:19:00.150 --> 01:19:02.790
and Frank Wilderson in the
beginning of his writings

01:19:02.790 --> 01:19:04.500
on Afro-pessimism, right?

01:19:04.500 --> 01:19:07.470
And it was, you know, they were
clever, they were brilliant,

01:19:07.470 --> 01:19:09.030
they were ethically committed.

01:19:09.030 --> 01:19:10.642
But it was, I think this
is what you were saying,

01:19:10.642 --> 01:19:14.283
there's certain truths
that nobody wants to hear.

01:19:15.450 --> 01:19:17.430
It's not even that they're inconvenient.

01:19:17.430 --> 01:19:20.970
They're incompatible with who we are.

01:19:20.970 --> 01:19:23.790
But now there's more compatibility.

01:19:23.790 --> 01:19:26.604
And so I'm wondering if they're
turning into commodities?

01:19:26.604 --> 01:19:31.200
If past rebels are now the
commodities of the 21st century?

01:19:31.200 --> 01:19:34.530
And if we're going down that road,

01:19:34.530 --> 01:19:38.343
then what is rebellion
against a proto fascist state,

01:19:39.270 --> 01:19:40.950
which the prison already is?

01:19:40.950 --> 01:19:43.980
You're just waiting for prison
culture, which is already,

01:19:43.980 --> 01:19:46.736
you know, it's a parallel
mirror, a distorted one,

01:19:46.736 --> 01:19:50.100
of increasingly what happens
at the borders, you know,

01:19:50.100 --> 01:19:52.800
when you shoot people
coming over, you know,

01:19:52.800 --> 01:19:56.430
'cause they're trying to
flee, you know, devastation.

01:19:56.430 --> 01:19:57.473
And increasingly what's happening

01:19:57.473 --> 01:20:02.250
in different sectors of society,
you know, Buffalo, Uvalde.

01:20:02.250 --> 01:20:05.970
So I mean, these are
way too big questions.

01:20:05.970 --> 01:20:06.817
They're giving me a headache,

01:20:06.817 --> 01:20:11.817
but they're constant and I
don't live the conditions.

01:20:12.930 --> 01:20:17.250
There are people who live the
conditions of severe violence

01:20:17.250 --> 01:20:19.980
who are thinking all the time.

01:20:19.980 --> 01:20:22.800
And then I think about it as an ally,

01:20:22.800 --> 01:20:25.890
but I'm not sure how
steady my bridge is over

01:20:25.890 --> 01:20:29.010
and I have no intention of
being implicated in anything

01:20:29.010 --> 01:20:32.220
so that I lose my
privilege or my standing.

01:20:32.220 --> 01:20:33.450
So those are some of...

01:20:33.450 --> 01:20:35.550
Like that's what I meant about the ethics.

01:20:35.550 --> 01:20:38.509
I'll say I'm a good person
so I have personal morality.

01:20:38.509 --> 01:20:42.360
What my political ethics
are, they continue to shift.

01:20:42.360 --> 01:20:44.550
I mean, one last thing is Kevin Johnson

01:20:44.550 --> 01:20:48.030
is supposed to be executed
in Missouri next month

01:20:48.030 --> 01:20:49.230
on November 29th.

01:20:49.230 --> 01:20:51.480
And so people have asked us to help,

01:20:51.480 --> 01:20:53.550
but you know, one, I don't
think, it's Missouri.

01:20:53.550 --> 01:20:55.290
I don't think he can stop this.

01:20:55.290 --> 01:20:58.710
But then what could be
said about his writings

01:20:58.710 --> 01:21:02.313
so that we remember him if
we can't keep him alive?

01:21:03.210 --> 01:21:05.550
And I think that's just
the basic bare minimum

01:21:05.550 --> 01:21:06.383
that people make.

01:21:06.383 --> 01:21:08.223
Those are their basic requests.

01:21:11.190 --> 01:21:12.510
- We have a few more minutes.

01:21:12.510 --> 01:21:14.980
I wonder if there's anyone in the audience

01:21:14.980 --> 01:21:17.580
with a question they are
interested in asking?

01:21:17.580 --> 01:21:19.380
I know it's always hard
to be the first person

01:21:19.380 --> 01:21:24.210
who asks a question, but it
looks like I have some takers.

01:21:24.210 --> 01:21:25.770
Peter, I feel like your hand is up.

01:21:25.770 --> 01:21:27.076
You get the first question.

01:21:27.076 --> 01:21:29.250
(Emily laughing)

01:21:29.250 --> 01:21:32.760
- [Peter] I was very struck
by all sorts of things,

01:21:32.760 --> 01:21:34.320
but by Rachel's phrase

01:21:34.320 --> 01:21:37.470
about being condemned to
religion and the Bible.

01:21:39.150 --> 01:21:41.070
I was struck by Rachel's phrase

01:21:41.070 --> 01:21:43.320
of being condemned to religion.

01:21:43.320 --> 01:21:45.209
The only book you can take into solitary

01:21:45.209 --> 01:21:49.263
is the Bible essentially,

01:21:50.400 --> 01:21:54.390
which aligns with the conversion narrative

01:21:54.390 --> 01:21:56.460
that Caleb was talking about.

01:21:56.460 --> 01:21:58.748
I was thinking back to an ancient genre

01:21:58.748 --> 01:22:03.748
I think starts in the 17th
century of the Newgate biography,

01:22:03.840 --> 01:22:05.400
where the Ordinary of Newgate,

01:22:05.400 --> 01:22:10.400
who was the chaplain of
a big prison in London,

01:22:11.280 --> 01:22:15.120
took confessions and autobiographies

01:22:15.120 --> 01:22:18.000
from convicts who were about to be hung.

01:22:18.000 --> 01:22:20.795
And he would literally stand
at the foot of the gallows

01:22:20.795 --> 01:22:24.210
and take their confessions
and then he'd write them up

01:22:24.210 --> 01:22:27.900
and publish them and
sell them for sixpence.

01:22:27.900 --> 01:22:30.930
And he made a handsome
supplement to his salary

01:22:30.930 --> 01:22:32.550
by doing this.

01:22:32.550 --> 01:22:36.012
So this is the moment of truth, right,

01:22:36.012 --> 01:22:40.031
in which they have to
express Christian thoughts,

01:22:40.031 --> 01:22:41.310
but it makes them,

01:22:41.310 --> 01:22:45.780
it gives them a kind of
posthumous glory in infamy

01:22:45.780 --> 01:22:48.750
because they're supposed
to detail all their crimes,

01:22:48.750 --> 01:22:52.230
but then tell how their
crimes led to their moment

01:22:52.230 --> 01:22:54.720
of repentance at the foot
of the gallows and so on.

01:22:54.720 --> 01:22:58.287
So there's some unhealthy
connection here, it seem to me,

01:22:58.287 --> 01:23:03.287
to conversion, infamy, and publicity,

01:23:05.759 --> 01:23:07.500
and profit of course

01:23:07.500 --> 01:23:09.228
'cause he's making a good profit at this.

01:23:09.228 --> 01:23:13.470
You talked about Vautrin,
Balzac's great criminal,

01:23:13.470 --> 01:23:15.480
and there's this wonderful moment

01:23:15.480 --> 01:23:17.460
when he converts to the police.

01:23:17.460 --> 01:23:20.168
And what he essentially says
is there's no difference

01:23:20.168 --> 01:23:21.930
between cops and robbers.

01:23:21.930 --> 01:23:23.980
There's just two faces of the same thing.

01:23:25.498 --> 01:23:26.331
- Can I?

01:23:26.331 --> 01:23:27.164
- Yeah.
- Okay.

01:23:28.290 --> 01:23:31.080
Yeah, listening to you talk, Peter,

01:23:31.080 --> 01:23:34.062
I was thinking also
about Joy's referencing

01:23:34.062 --> 01:23:36.453
of the death penalty.

01:23:39.390 --> 01:23:42.750
So like in this version where
you're allowed to confess

01:23:42.750 --> 01:23:45.413
or forced to confess right before you go,

01:23:45.413 --> 01:23:47.550
unfortunately I can't remember
the name of the person,

01:23:47.550 --> 01:23:52.240
but somebody was executed
just a couple weeks ago

01:23:52.240 --> 01:23:55.103
and the last line of the article was

01:23:55.103 --> 01:23:57.789
he said nothing when
offered a final comment,

01:23:57.789 --> 01:23:59.670
you know, before they,

01:23:59.670 --> 01:24:01.890
I can't remember if he
died by electrocution

01:24:01.890 --> 01:24:02.996
or by injection.

01:24:02.996 --> 01:24:05.532
One of those two methods.

01:24:05.532 --> 01:24:06.960
(audience member speaking off mic)

01:24:06.960 --> 01:24:07.980
Okay, thank you very much.

01:24:07.980 --> 01:24:09.270
Joe Nathan James.

01:24:09.270 --> 01:24:14.270
There was another execution
last summer, I believe.

01:24:15.120 --> 01:24:17.550
Unfortunately also, look,
I'm nervous up here.

01:24:17.550 --> 01:24:19.710
I don't remember the name
of that person either.

01:24:19.710 --> 01:24:22.290
But what the odd thing that I remember

01:24:22.290 --> 01:24:23.730
about this other execution

01:24:23.730 --> 01:24:27.051
is that the journalist who
was sent to report on it

01:24:27.051 --> 01:24:30.240
was told by the guards that
her skirt was not long enough

01:24:30.240 --> 01:24:33.570
and that it needed to
be exactly at the knee

01:24:33.570 --> 01:24:35.520
and not one inch above it.

01:24:35.520 --> 01:24:38.101
I believe it was half
an inch above her knee.

01:24:38.101 --> 01:24:40.530
And so one of the other,

01:24:40.530 --> 01:24:42.977
one of the lawyers who was
gonna be at that execution

01:24:42.977 --> 01:24:45.630
loaned her from the trunk of his car

01:24:45.630 --> 01:24:48.690
a pair of fishing hip waders,

01:24:48.690 --> 01:24:53.070
which she was allowed to
wear into this execution.

01:24:53.070 --> 01:24:54.030
The reason I bring this up

01:24:54.030 --> 01:24:56.880
is that it just speaks to the absurdity

01:24:56.880 --> 01:24:58.860
of the bureaucratic apparatus

01:24:58.860 --> 01:25:03.860
that attends to matters of life
and death involving people.

01:25:04.830 --> 01:25:07.770
I think about that a
lot, how strange that is.

01:25:07.770 --> 01:25:10.330
I guess finally, maybe I just wanna add

01:25:16.110 --> 01:25:17.670
that these conversions,

01:25:17.670 --> 01:25:19.096
that these confessions, excuse me,

01:25:19.096 --> 01:25:21.930
were sold, according to Peter,

01:25:21.930 --> 01:25:25.410
is interesting to me because it also maybe

01:25:25.410 --> 01:25:26.365
reminds me of a point

01:25:26.365 --> 01:25:29.127
that maybe I keep coming
back to here this morning

01:25:29.127 --> 01:25:33.695
about some desperate need on
the part of a free citizenry

01:25:33.695 --> 01:25:35.949
or those who manage a free citizenry

01:25:35.949 --> 01:25:39.075
to keep separate those who need to confess

01:25:39.075 --> 01:25:42.360
from those who might possess a confession

01:25:42.360 --> 01:25:44.910
almost as a talismanic reminder

01:25:44.910 --> 01:25:49.910
that they themselves will not
be entangled in that process.

01:25:52.025 --> 01:25:54.575
- [Audience Member] He has
this beautiful passage in this

01:25:54.575 --> 01:25:57.672
and remarks on Marx from
'78 where he says, well,

01:25:57.672 --> 01:26:02.340
of course all of my work has
always been fiction, right?

01:26:02.340 --> 01:26:04.410
How could you not do fiction?

01:26:04.410 --> 01:26:05.940
My historical work was fiction.

01:26:05.940 --> 01:26:08.784
Of course historical facts,
you can check them all.

01:26:08.784 --> 01:26:13.050
You can do the kind of
fact-checking for that work

01:26:13.050 --> 01:26:17.490
and for social scientific work,
but threading it together,

01:26:17.490 --> 01:26:19.410
sewing it together, right,

01:26:19.410 --> 01:26:23.970
and what you do with it is
always an act of fiction.

01:26:23.970 --> 01:26:28.443
And in part we aren't
willing, I mean, that's true.

01:26:29.460 --> 01:26:34.460
Our nonfiction is fiction, our
social sciences are fiction,

01:26:35.349 --> 01:26:39.420
but we're not willing
in part to accept that

01:26:39.420 --> 01:26:43.719
because as academics we need
to protect ourselves from that,

01:26:43.719 --> 01:26:46.413
from that cut.

01:26:47.265 --> 01:26:51.693
But in truth, it's true
of journalism as well.

01:26:54.203 --> 01:26:56.190
Everything is fiction,

01:26:56.190 --> 01:26:59.605
and what that means is that everything

01:26:59.605 --> 01:27:01.533
is a political engagement.

01:27:04.170 --> 01:27:06.300
- Yes, why don't you have the last word

01:27:06.300 --> 01:27:07.980
'cause I wanna be respectful of the time.

01:27:07.980 --> 01:27:09.087
I'll close things off.
- Oh no, dear.

01:27:09.087 --> 01:27:09.920
- No, no, sorry,
- I'll be really--

01:27:09.920 --> 01:27:12.450
- I didn't mean to intimidate you, go.

01:27:12.450 --> 01:27:14.040
- Well I love what you said,

01:27:14.040 --> 01:27:15.960
but I also fear that maybe like fiction

01:27:15.960 --> 01:27:17.400
is a kind of dirty word there

01:27:17.400 --> 01:27:19.980
because it suggests a straying from truth.

01:27:19.980 --> 01:27:22.200
Although you probably meant something

01:27:22.200 --> 01:27:23.760
much more nuanced than that.

01:27:23.760 --> 01:27:28.760
But I would say like
fiction to me in a way

01:27:29.060 --> 01:27:32.700
is actually sort of,
back to your question,

01:27:32.700 --> 01:27:36.090
is separate from or safe
from or protected from

01:27:36.090 --> 01:27:38.410
in a sense the political

01:27:39.270 --> 01:27:41.970
because the writer herself,

01:27:41.970 --> 01:27:45.407
when she embarks on the
project of reflecting the world

01:27:45.407 --> 01:27:48.429
through the grand and
hallucinatory mythical envelope

01:27:48.429 --> 01:27:52.385
that we call the novel and
started, you know, 200 years ago,

01:27:52.385 --> 01:27:53.883
when she embarks on that project,

01:27:53.883 --> 01:27:57.990
she does not already have
an idea or an argument

01:27:57.990 --> 01:28:01.470
of what the problem is
nor what the solution is,

01:28:01.470 --> 01:28:06.470
and rather wants to reflect
reality in its granular detail

01:28:06.840 --> 01:28:09.229
to the best of her ability.

01:28:09.229 --> 01:28:13.590
So in that sense, if she has
somebody who's in prison,

01:28:13.590 --> 01:28:14.617
if they're in prison in California,

01:28:14.617 --> 01:28:17.190
probably they actually did the thing

01:28:17.190 --> 01:28:18.420
that they were convicted of

01:28:18.420 --> 01:28:21.450
and she's not going for what
the reader would like to hear,

01:28:21.450 --> 01:28:24.180
which is that this person
is in fact innocent.

01:28:24.180 --> 01:28:25.408
And she's gonna need to be able to

01:28:25.408 --> 01:28:29.760
ensnare some of the
absurdity of the institution.

01:28:29.760 --> 01:28:31.710
Like they give you a
handbook that's 30 pages

01:28:31.710 --> 01:28:33.450
and then they give you
a guide to the handbook

01:28:33.450 --> 01:28:36.123
that's 140 pages and they
want you to read both.

01:28:37.710 --> 01:28:39.600
And then I guess just,
well, I don't wanna close,

01:28:39.600 --> 01:28:40.433
so why don't you.

01:28:40.433 --> 01:28:43.708
I just wanted to say that I do think

01:28:43.708 --> 01:28:48.518
that I see the novel as
not necessarily political.

01:28:48.518 --> 01:28:50.280
That doesn't mean that I'm not political.

01:28:50.280 --> 01:28:54.180
I spent two years
writing a very long piece

01:28:54.180 --> 01:28:56.880
about prison abolition for
"The New York Times Magazine."

01:28:56.880 --> 01:28:58.695
So that's like a different set of tools

01:28:58.695 --> 01:29:02.370
that I use on occasion,
but it's not the novel.

01:29:02.370 --> 01:29:03.210
So I just want...

01:29:03.210 --> 01:29:04.290
- [Joy] Can we say--

01:29:04.290 --> 01:29:05.703
- [Emily] Yes, please.

01:29:07.320 --> 01:29:09.480
- Yeah, so again, like thanks everybody,

01:29:09.480 --> 01:29:11.190
but I do wanna echo what's been said.

01:29:11.190 --> 01:29:12.810
I think the politics are,

01:29:12.810 --> 01:29:15.030
that's the stumbling block.

01:29:15.030 --> 01:29:18.377
And also, you know, I believe
in emotional intelligence.

01:29:18.377 --> 01:29:21.510
And so if you accept the politics,

01:29:21.510 --> 01:29:24.030
then you accept certain layers of trauma

01:29:24.030 --> 01:29:27.420
even if it's secondary or
tertiary 'cause you're not inside.

01:29:27.420 --> 01:29:30.450
And I think that also
can sometimes inhibit

01:29:30.450 --> 01:29:32.910
whether or not we will
walk closer to politics

01:29:32.910 --> 01:29:34.203
that are not popular.

01:29:35.340 --> 01:29:40.340
So to shorten, I would
say I create two personas.

01:29:40.380 --> 01:29:42.058
I do the political work.

01:29:42.058 --> 01:29:44.730
I use the academy to pay for me,

01:29:44.730 --> 01:29:47.293
you know, my family and health insurance.

01:29:47.293 --> 01:29:50.658
And then I write theory as an academic.

01:29:50.658 --> 01:29:55.020
I do the anthologies as a side gig,

01:29:55.020 --> 01:29:57.814
which is where my heart is.

01:29:57.814 --> 01:30:00.150
And that's the balance.

01:30:00.150 --> 01:30:01.438
If you wanna call me a writer,

01:30:01.438 --> 01:30:03.266
you know, I'm definitely an academic,

01:30:03.266 --> 01:30:08.266
but anything I edit is because
there's a relationship.

01:30:08.790 --> 01:30:10.920
Like all the relationships
that you expressed,

01:30:10.920 --> 01:30:12.252
we all have them.

01:30:12.252 --> 01:30:14.700
It's to show up to the relationship,

01:30:14.700 --> 01:30:17.790
which then becomes a form of politics.

01:30:17.790 --> 01:30:19.950
- That seems like an
excellent note to close on.

01:30:19.950 --> 01:30:21.313
I feel like there's so many ideas

01:30:21.313 --> 01:30:23.300
which we can spend the day unpacking.

01:30:23.300 --> 01:30:25.980
Thank you so much,
Rachel and Caleb and Joy.

01:30:25.980 --> 01:30:26.813
Thank you.

01:30:26.813 --> 01:30:30.999
(audience and panelists applauding)

01:30:33.679 --> 01:30:36.179
(light music)

